(n_samples_Y, n_features), default=None\n1247 |         An optional second feature array. If `None`, uses `Y=X`.\n1248 | \n1249 |     dense_output : bool, default=True\n1250 |         Whether to return dense output even when the input is sparse. If\n1251 |         ``False``, the output is sparse if both input arrays are sparse.\n1252 | \n1253 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n1254 | \n1255 |     Returns\n1256 |     -------\n1257 |     Gram matrix : ndarray of shape (n_samples_X, n_samples_Y)\n1258 |         The Gram matrix of the linear kernel, i.e. `X @ Y.T`.\n1259 |     \"\"\"\n1260 |     X, Y = check_pairwise_arrays(X, Y)\n1261 |     return safe_sparse_dot(X, Y.T, dense_output=dense_output)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/preprocessing/_data.py:binarize`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2030 | def binarize(X, *, threshold=0.0, copy=True):\n2031 |     \"\"\"Boolean thresholding of array-like or scipy.sparse matrix.\n2032 | \n2033 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <preprocessing_binarization>`.\n2034 | \n2035 |     Parameters\n2036 |     ----------\n2037 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n2038 |         The data to binarize, element by element.\n2039 |         scipy.sparse matrices should be in CSR or CSC format to avoid an\n2040 |         un-necessary copy.\n2041 | \n2042 |     threshold : float, default=0.0\n2043 |         Feature values below or equal to this are replaced by 0, above it by 1.\n2044 |         Threshold may not be less than 0 for operations on sparse matrices.\n2045 | \n2046 |     copy : bool, default=True\n2047 |         Set to False to perform inplace binarization and avoid a copy\n2048 |         (if the input is already a numpy array or a scipy.sparse CSR / CSC\n2049 |         matrix and if axis is 1).\n2050 | \n2051 |     Returns\n2052 |     -------\n2053 |     X_tr : {ndarray, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n2054 |         The transformed data.\n2055 | \n2056 |     See Also\n2057 |     --------\n2058 |     Binarizer : Performs binarization using the Transformer API\n2059 |         (e.g. as part of a preprocessing :class:`~sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline`).\n2060 |     \"\"\"\n2061 |     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=[\"csr\", \"csc\"], copy=copy)\n2062 |     if sparse.issparse(X):\n2063 |         if threshold < 0:\n2064 |             raise ValueError(\"Cannot binarize a sparse matrix with threshold < 0\")\n2065 |         cond = X.data > threshold\n2066 |         not_cond = np.logical_not(cond)\n2067 |         X.data[cond] = 1\n2068 |         X.data[not_cond] = 0\n2069 |         X.eliminate_zeros()\n2070 |     else:\n2071 |         cond = X > threshold\n2072 |         not_cond = np.logical_not(cond)\n2073 |         X[cond] = 1\n2074 |         X[not_cond] = 0\n2075 |     return X\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"design_matrix sparse\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | ==============================\n 3 | Lasso on dense and sparse data\n 4 | ==============================\n 5 | \n 6 | We show that linear_model.Lasso provides the same results for dense and sparse\n 7 | data and that in the case of sparse data the speed is improved.\n 8 | \n 9 | \"\"\"\n10 | \n11 | from time import time\n12 | from scipy import sparse\n13 | from scipy import linalg\n14 | \n15 | from sklearn.datasets import make_regression\n16 | from sklearn.linear_model import Lasso\n17 | \n18 | \n19 | # %%\n20 | # Comparing the two Lasso implementations on Dense data\n21 | # -----------------------------------------------------\n22 | #\n23 | # We create a linear regression problem that is suitable for the Lasso,\n24 | # that is to say, with more features than samples. We then store the data\n25 | # matrix in both dense (the usual) and sparse format, and train a Lasso on\n26 | # each. We compute the runtime of both and check that they learned the\n27 | # same model by computing the Euclidean norm of the difference between the\n28 | # coefficients they learned. Because the data is dense, we expect better\n29 | # runtime with a dense data format.\n30 | \n31 | X, y = make_regression(n_samples=200, n_features=5000, random_state=0)\n32 | # create a copy of X in sparse format\n33 | X_sp = sparse.coo_matrix(X)\n34 | \n35 | alpha = 1\n36 | sparse_lasso = Lasso(alpha=alpha, fit_intercept=False, max_iter=1000)\n37 | dense_lasso = Lasso(alpha=alpha, fit_intercept=False, max_iter=1000)\n38 | \n39 | t0 = time()\n40 | sparse_lasso.fit(X_sp, y)\n41 | print(f\"Sparse Lasso done in {(time() - t0):.3f}s\")\n42 | \n43 | t0 = time()\n44 | dense_lasso.fit(X, y)\n45 | print(f\"Dense Lasso done in {(time() - t0):.3f}s\")\n46 | \n47 | # compare the regression coefficients\n48 | coeff_diff = linalg.norm(sparse_lasso.coef_ - dense_lasso.coef_)\n49 | print(f\"Distance between coefficients : {coeff_diff:.2e}\")\n50 | \n51 | #\n52 | # %%\n53 | # Comparing the two Lasso implementations on Sparse data\n54 | # ------------------------------------------------------\n55 | #\n56 | # We make the previous problem sparse by replacing all small values with 0\n57 | # and run the same comparisons as above. Because the data is now sparse, we\n58 | # expect the implementation that uses the sparse data format to be faster.\n59 | \n60 | # make a copy of the previous data\n61 | Xs = X.copy()\n62 | # make Xs sparse by replacing the values lower than 2.5 with 0s\n63 | Xs[Xs < 2.5] = 0.0\n64 | # create a copy of Xs in sparse format\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_pairwise_distances_reduction/_dispatcher.py:BaseDistancesReductionDispatcher.is_usable_for.is_valid_sparse_matrix`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n107 | def is_valid_sparse_matrix(X):\n108 |             return (\n109 |                 isspmatrix_csr(X)\n110 |                 and\n111 |                 # TODO: support CSR matrices without non-zeros elements\n112 |                 X.nnz > 0\n113 |                 and\n114 |                 # TODO: support CSR matrices with int64 indices and indptr\n115 |                 # See: https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/issues/23653\n116 |                 X.indices.dtype == X.indptr.dtype == np.int32\n117 |             )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_samples_generator.py:make_sparse_spd_matrix`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1559 | def make_sparse_spd_matrix(\n1560 |     dim=1,\n1561 |     *,\n1562 |     alpha=0.95,\n1563 |     norm_diag=False,\n1564 |     smallest_coef=0.1,\n1565 |     largest_coef=0.9,\n1566 |     random_state=None,\n1567 | ):\n1568 |     \"\"\"Generate a sparse symmetric definite positive matrix.\n1569 | \n1570 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <sample_generators>`.\n1571 | \n1572 |     Parameters\n1573 |     ----------\n1574 |     dim : int, default=1\n1575 |         The size of the random matrix to generate.\n1576 | \n1577 |     alpha : float, default=0.95\n1578 |         The probability that a coefficient is zero (see notes). Larger values\n1579 |         enforce more sparsity. The value should be in the range 0 and 1.\n1580 | \n1581 |     norm_diag : bool, default=False\n1582 |         Whether to normalize the output matrix to make the leading diagonal\n1583 |         elements all 1.\n1584 | \n1585 |     smallest_coef : float, default=0.1\n1586 |         The value of the smallest coefficient between 0 and 1.\n1587 | \n1588 |     largest_coef : float, default=0.9\n1589 |         The value of the largest coefficient between 0 and 1.\n1590 | \n1591 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1592 |         Determines random number generation for dataset creation. Pass an int\n1593 |         for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n1594 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1595 | \n1596 |     Returns\n1597 |     -------\n1598 |     prec : sparse matrix of shape (dim, dim)\n1599 |         The generated matrix.\n1600 | \n1601 |     See Also\n1602 |     --------\n1603 |     make_spd_matrix : Generate a random symmetric, positive-definite matrix.\n1604 | \n1605 |     Notes\n1606 |     -----\n1607 |     The sparsity is actually imposed on the cholesky factor of the matrix.\n1608 |     Thus alpha does not translate directly into the filling fraction of\n1609 |     the matrix itself.\n1610 |     \"\"\"\n1611 |     random_state = check_random_state(random_state)\n1612 | \n1613 |     chol = -np.eye(dim)\n1614 |     aux = random_state.uniform(size=(dim, dim))\n1615 |     aux[aux < alpha] = 0\n1616 |     aux[aux > alpha] = smallest_coef + (\n1617 |         largest_coef - smallest_coef\n1618 |     ) * random_state.uniform(size=np.sum(aux > alpha))\n1619 |     aux = np.tril(aux, k=-1)\n1620 | \n1621 |     # Permute the lines: we don't want to have asymmetries in the final\n1622 |     # SPD matrix\n1623 |     permutation = random_state.permutation(dim)\n1624 |     aux = aux[permutation].T[permutation]\n1625 |     chol += aux\n1626 |     prec = np.dot(chol.T, chol)\n1627 | \n1628 |     if norm_diag:\n1629 |         # Form the diagonal vector into a row matrix\n1630 |         d = np.diag(prec).reshape(1, prec.shape[0])\n1631 |         d = 1.0 / np.sqrt(d)\n1632 | \n1633 |         prec *= d\n1634 |         prec *= d.T\n1635 | \n1636 |     return prec\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let's also check if there are any usage examples or test files:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['test_spline_transformer', 'SplineTransformer']</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>**/test_*.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nNo files found for file pattern '**/test_*.py'. Will search all files.\n...\nNo results for test files: `test_spline_transformer`. Please do not search for any test files.\n