    feature_names = None\n1616 | \n1617 |     # extract feature names for support array containers\n1618 |     if hasattr(X, \"columns\"):\n1619 |         feature_names = np.asarray(X.columns)\n1620 | \n1621 |     if feature_names is None or len(feature_names) == 0:\n1622 |         return\n1623 | \n1624 |     types = sorted(t.__qualname__ for t in set(type(v) for v in feature_names))\n1625 | \n1626 |     # Warn when types are mixed.\n1627 |     # ints and strings do not warn\n1628 |     if len(types) > 1 or not (types[0].startswith(\"int\") or types[0] == \"str\"):\n1629 |         # TODO: Convert to an error in 1.2\n1630 |         warnings.warn(\n1631 |             \"Feature names only support names that are all strings. \"\n1632 |             f\"Got feature names with dtypes: {types}. An error will be raised \"\n1633 |             \"in 1.2.\",\n1634 |             FutureWarning,\n1635 |         )\n1636 |         return\n1637 | \n1638 |     # Only feature names of all strings are supported\n1639 |     if types[0] == \"str\":\n1640 |         return feature_names\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_base.py:load_diabetes`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 911 | def load_diabetes(*, return_X_y=False, as_frame=False):\n 912 |     \"\"\"Load and return the diabetes dataset (regression).\n 913 | \n 914 |     ==============   ==================\n 915 |     Samples total    442\n 916 |     Dimensionality   10\n 917 |     Features         real, -.2 < x < .2\n 918 |     Targets          integer 25 - 346\n 919 |     ==============   ==================\n 920 | \n 921 |     .. note::\n 922 |        The meaning of each feature (i.e. `feature_names`) might be unclear\n 923 |        (especially for `ltg`) as the documentation of the original dataset is\n 924 |        not explicit. We provide information that seems correct in regard with\n 925 |        the scientific literature in this field of research.\n 926 | \n 927 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <diabetes_dataset>`.\n 928 | \n 929 |     Parameters\n 930 |     ----------\n 931 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False.\n 932 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object.\n 933 |         See below for more information about the `data` and `target` object.\n 934 | \n 935 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 936 | \n 937 |     as_frame : bool, default=False\n 938 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n 939 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric). The target is\n 940 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target columns.\n 941 |         If `return_X_y` is True, then (`data`, `target`) will be pandas\n 942 |         DataFrames or Series as described below.\n 943 | \n 944 |         .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 945 | \n 946 |     Returns\n 947 |     -------\n 948 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n 949 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n 950 | \n 951 |         data : {ndarray, dataframe} of shape (442, 10)\n 952 |             The data matrix. If `as_frame=True`, `data` will be a pandas\n 953 |             DataFrame.\n 954 |         target: {ndarray, Series} of shape (442,)\n 955 |             The regression target. If `as_frame=True`, `target` will be\n 956 |             a pandas Series.\n 957 |         feature_names: list\n 958 |             The names of the dataset columns.\n 959 |         frame: DataFrame of shape (442, 11)\n 960 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with `data` and\n 961 |             `target`.\n 962 | \n 963 |             .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 964 |         DESCR: str\n 965 |             The full description of the dataset.\n 966 |         data_filename: str\n 967 |             The path to the location of the data.\n 968 |         target_filename: str\n 969 |             The path to the location of the target.\n 970 | \n 971 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n 972 | \n 973 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 974 |     \"\"\"\n 975 |     data_filename = \"diabetes_data.csv.gz\"\n 976 |     target_filename = \"diabetes_target.csv.gz\"\n 977 |     data = load_gzip_compressed_csv_data(data_filename)\n 978 |     target = load_gzip_compressed_csv_data(target_filename)\n 979 | \n 980 |     fdescr = load_descr(\"diabetes.rst\")\n 981 | \n 982 |     feature_names = [\"age\", \"sex\", \"bmi\", \"bp\", \"s1\", \"s2\", \"s3\", \"s4\", \"s5\", \"s6\"]\n 983 | \n 984 |     frame = None\n 985 |     target_columns = [\n 986 |         \"target\",\n 987 |     ]\n 988 |     if as_frame:\n 989 |         frame, data, target = _convert_data_dataframe(\n 990 |             \"load_diabetes\", data, target, feature_names, target_columns\n 991 |         )\n 992 | \n 993 |     if return_X_y:\n 994 |         return data, target\n 995 | \n 996 |     return Bunch(\n 997 |         data=data,\n 998 |         target=target,\n 999 |         frame=frame,\n1000 |         DESCR=fdescr,\n1001 |         feature_names=feature_names,\n1002 |         data_filename=data_filename,\n1003 |         target_filename=target_filename,\n1004 |         data_module=DATA_MODULE,\n1005 |     )\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let's also check for any plotting utility code that might be relevant:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['plot', 'matplotlib']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/utils/__init__.py:check_matplotlib_support\nsklearn/conftest.py:pyplot\nsklearn/conftest.py:pytest_configure\nsklearn/conftest.py:pytest_runtest_setup\nsklearn/inspection/_plot/partial_dependence.py:PartialDependenceDisplay.plot\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay.plot\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay.__init__\n##Searching for term \"plot\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/det_curve.py:DetCurveDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py:RocCurveDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py:ConfusionMatrixDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/inspection/_plot/partial_dependence.py:PartialDependenceDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `benchmarks/bench_plot_parallel_pairwise.py:plot`.\nFound function `benchmarks/bench_saga.py:plot`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `plot`.\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"matplotlib\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `examples/applications/svm_gui.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ==========\n  3 | Libsvm GUI\n  4 | ==========\n  5 | \n  6 | A simple graphical frontend for Libsvm mainly intended for didactic\n  7 | purposes. You can create data points by point and click and visualize\n  8 | the decision region induced by different kernels and parameter settings.\n  9 | \n 10 | To create positive examples click the left mouse button; to create\n 11 | negative examples click the right button.\n 12 | \n 13 | If all examples are from the same class, it uses a one-class SVM.\n 14 | \n 15 | \"\"\"\n 16 | \n 17 | print(__doc__)\n 18 | \n 19 | # Author: Peter Prettenhoer <peter.prettenhofer@gmail.com>\n 20 | #\n 21 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n 22 | \n 23 | import matplotlib\n 24 | matplotlib.use('TkAgg')\n 25 | from matplotlib.backends.backend_tkagg import FigureCanvasTkAgg\n 26 | try:\n 27 |     from matplotlib.backends.backend_tkagg import NavigationToolbar2Tk\n 28 | except ImportError:\n 29 |     # NavigationToolbar2TkAgg was deprecated in matplotlib 2.2\n 30 |     from matplotlib.backends.backend_tkagg import (\n 31 |         NavigationToolbar2TkAgg as NavigationToolbar2Tk\n 32 |     )\n 33 | from matplotlib.figure import Figure\n 34 | from matplotlib.contour import ContourSet\n 35 | \n 36 | import sys\n 37 | import numpy as np\n 38 | import tkinter as Tk\n 39 | \n 40 | from sklearn import svm\n 41 | from sklearn.datasets import dump_svmlight_file\n 42 | \n 43 | y_min, y_max = -50, 50\n 44 | x_min, x_max = -50, 50\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/__init__.py:check_matplotlib_support`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1085 | def check_matplotlib_support(caller_name):\n1086 |     \"\"\"Raise ImportError with detailed error message if mpl is not installed.\n1087 | \n1088 |     Plot utilities like :func:`plot_partial_dependence` should lazily import\n1089 |     matplotlib and call this helper before any computation.\n1090 | \n1091 |     Parameters\n1092 |     ----------\n1093 |     caller_name : str\n1094 |         The name of the caller that requires matplotlib.\n1095 |     \"\"\"\n1096 |     try:\n1097 |         import matplotlib  # noqa\n1098 |     except ImportError as e:\n1099 |         raise ImportError(\n1100 |             \"{} requires matplotlib. You can install matplotlib with \"\n1101 |             \"`pip install matplotlib`\".format(caller_name)\n1102 |         ) from e\n```\n\nFound function `doc/conftest.py:skip_if_matplotlib_not_installed`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n102 | def skip_if_matplotlib_not_installed(fname):\n103 |     try:\n104 |         import matplotlib  # noqa\n105 |     except ImportError:\n106 |         basename = os.path.basename(fname)\n107 |         raise SkipTest(f\"Skipping doctests for {basename}, matplotlib not installed\")\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/conftest.py:pyplot`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n179 | def pyplot():\n180 |     \"\"\"Setup and teardown fixture for matplotlib.\n181 | \n182 |     This fixture checks if we can import matplotlib. If not, the tests will be\n183 |     skipped. Otherwise, we close the figures before and after running the\n184 |     functions.\n185 | \n186 |     Returns\n187 |     -------\n188 |     pyplot : module\n189 |         The ``matplotlib.pyplot`` module.\n190 |     \"\"\"\n191 |     pyplot = pytest.importorskip(\"matplotlib.pyplot\")\n192 |     pyplot.close(\"all\")\n193 |     yield pyplot\n194 |     pyplot.close(\"all\")\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/conftest.py:pytest_runtest_setup`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n197 | def pytest_runtest_setup(item):\n198 |     \"\"\"Set the number of openmp threads based on the number of workers\n199 |     xdist is using to prevent oversubscription.\n200 | \n201 |     Parameters\n202 |     ----------\n203 |     item : pytest item\n204 |         item to be processed\n205 |     \"\"\"\n206 |     xdist_worker_count = environ.get(\"PYTEST_XDIST_WORKER_COUNT\")\n207 |     if xdist_worker_count is None:\n208 |         # returns if pytest-xdist is not installed\n209 |         return\n210 |     else:\n211 |         xdist_worker_count = int(xdist_worker_count)\n212 | \n213 |     openmp_threads = _openmp_effective_n_threads()\n214 |     threads_per_worker = max(openmp_threads // xdist_worker_count, 1)\n215 |     threadpool_limits(threads_per_worker, user_api=\"openmp\")\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/conftest.py:pytest_configure`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n218 | def pytest_configure(config):\n219 |     # Use matplotlib agg backend during the tests including doctests\n220 |     try:\n221 |         import matplotlib\n222 | \n223 |         matplotlib.use(\"agg\")\n224 |     except ImportError:\n225 |         pass\n```\n\nFound file `sklearn/utils/extmath.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `sklearn/utils/extmath.py` for the full content if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Based on my analysis, here are the key locations that need to be modified to implement the requested calibration curve plotting feature:\n\n```\nsklearn/calibration.py\nline: 840-945\nfunction: calibration_curve\n\nsklearn/calibration.py\nline: 843-948\nfunction: calibration_curve\n\nsklearn/utils/__init__.py\nline: 1085-1102\nfunction: check_matplotlib_support\n\nsklearn/calibration.py\nline: 227-241\nclass: CalibratedClassifierCV\nfunction: __init__\n\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py\nline: 72-126\nfunction: plot_calibration_curve\n```\n\nExplanation of why these locations are relevant:\n\n1. `sklearn/calibration.py`: This is the main file containing the calibration functionality. The `calibration_curve` function is already implemented here (lines 840-948), which will be the foundation for the new plotting function. The `CalibratedClassifierCV` class also provides useful context about how the calibration works and what inputs are expected.\n\n2. `sklearn/utils/__init__.py`: Contains the `check_matplotlib_support` function that needs to be used to verify matplotlib availability before plotting.\n\n3. The example file `examples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py` shows an existing calibration curve plotting implementation that follows the same pattern as requested feature.\n\nThese locations represent form the complete set needed to implement the requested feature:\n- The core calibration curve computation is already implemented\n- The plotting utility functions exist in the calibration module\n- The example demonstrates the correct usage patterns\n- All necessary supporting functions are available for the implementation\n\nThe implementation would need to focus on creating a new function in sklearn/calibration.py that wraps the existing calibration_curve functionality and adds the plotting logic, following the same pattern as the example in examples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "user", "content": "Verify if the found locations contain all the necessary information to address the is