ics\" with function pls(X, Y, mode = \"regression\")\n    #     - \"plspm \" with function plsreg2(X, Y)\n    #     - \"pls\" with function oscorespls.fit(X, Y)\n\n    def __init__(\n        self, n_components=2, *, scale=True, max_iter=500, tol=1e-06, copy=True\n    ):\n...\n    def fit(self, X, Y):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound class `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass PLSCanonical(_PLS):\n\n    # This implementation provides the same results that the \"plspm\" package\n    # provided in the R language (R-project), using the function plsca(X, Y).\n    # Results are equal or collinear with the function\n    # ``pls(..., mode = \"canonical\")`` of the \"mixOmics\" package. The\n    # difference relies in the fact that mixOmics implementation does not\n    # exactly implement the Wold algorithm since it does not normalize\n    # y_weights to one.\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        n_components=2,\n        *,\n        scale=True,\n        algorithm=\"nipals\",\n        max_iter=500,\n        tol=1e-06,\n        copy=True,\n    ):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:check_non_negative`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1336 | def check_non_negative(X, whom):\n1337 |     \"\"\"\n1338 |     Check if there is any negative value in an array.\n1339 | \n1340 |     Parameters\n1341 |     ----------\n1342 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix}\n1343 |         Input data.\n1344 | \n1345 |     whom : str\n1346 |         Who passed X to this function.\n1347 |     \"\"\"\n1348 |     # avoid X.min() on sparse matrix since it also sorts the indices\n1349 |     if sp.issparse(X):\n1350 |         if X.format in [\"lil\", \"dok\"]:\n1351 |             X = X.tocsr()\n1352 |         if X.data.size == 0:\n1353 |             X_min = 0\n1354 |         else:\n1355 |             X_min = X.data.min()\n1356 |     else:\n1357 |         X_min = X.min()\n1358 | \n1359 |     if X_min < 0:\n1360 |         raise ValueError(\"Negative values in data passed to %s\" % whom)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:_generate_get_feature_names_out`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1918 | def _generate_get_feature_names_out(estimator, n_features_out, input_features=None):\n1919 |     \"\"\"Generate feature names out for estimator using the estimator name as the prefix.\n1920 | \n1921 |     The input_feature names are validated but not used. This function is useful\n1922 |     for estimators that generate their own names based on `n_features_out`, i.e. PCA.\n1923 | \n1924 |     Parameters\n1925 |     ----------\n1926 |     estimator : estimator instance\n1927 |         Estimator producing output feature names.\n1928 | \n1929 |     n_feature_out : int\n1930 |         Number of feature names out.\n1931 | \n1932 |     input_features : array-like of str or None, default=None\n1933 |         Only used to validate feature names with `estimator.feature_names_in_`.\n1934 | \n1935 |     Returns\n1936 |     -------\n1937 |     feature_names_in : ndarray of str or `None`\n1938 |         Feature names in.\n1939 |     \"\"\"\n1940 |     _check_feature_names_in(estimator, input_features, generate_names=False)\n1941 |     estimator_name = estimator.__class__.__name__.lower()\n1942 |     return np.asarray(\n1943 |         [f\"{estimator_name}{i}\" for i in range(n_features_out)], dtype=object\n1944 |     )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/neighbors/_lof.py:LocalOutlierFactor.fit`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n258 | def fit(self, X, y=None):\n259 |         \"\"\"Fit the local outlier factor detector from the training dataset.\n260 | \n261 |         Parameters\n262 |         ----------\n263 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features) or \\\n264 |                 (n_samples, n_samples) if metric='precomputed'\n265 |             Training data.\n266 | \n267 |         y : Ignored\n268 |             Not used, present for API consistency by convention.\n269 | \n270 |         Returns\n271 |         -------\n272 |         self : LocalOutlierFactor\n273 |             The fitted local outlier factor detector.\n274 |         \"\"\"\n275 |         self._fit(X)\n276 | \n277 |         if self.contamination != \"auto\":\n278 |             if not (0.0 < self.contamination <= 0.5):\n279 |                 raise ValueError(\n280 |                     \"contamination must be in (0, 0.5], got: %f\" % self.contamination\n281 |                 )\n282 | \n283 |         n_samples = self.n_samples_fit_\n284 |         if self.n_neighbors > n_samples:\n285 |             warnings.warn(\n286 |                 \"n_neighbors (%s) is greater than the \"\n287 |                 \"total number of samples (%s). n_neighbors \"\n288 |                 \"will be set to (n_samples - 1) for estimation.\"\n289 |                 % (self.n_neighbors, n_samples)\n290 |             )\n291 |         self.n_neighbors_ = max(1, min(self.n_neighbors, n_samples - 1))\n292 | \n293 |         self._distances_fit_X_, _neighbors_indices_fit_X_ = self.kneighbors(\n294 |             n_neighbors=self.n_neighbors_\n295 |         )\n296 | \n297 |         self._lrd = self._local_reachability_density(\n298 |             self._distances_fit_X_, _neighbors_indices_fit_X_\n299 |         )\n300 | \n301 |         # Compute lof score over training samples to define offset_:\n302 |         lrd_ratios_array = (\n303 |             self._lrd[_neighbors_indices_fit_X_] / self._lrd[:, np.newaxis]\n304 |         )\n305 | \n306 |         self.negative_outlier_factor_ = -np.mean(lrd_ratios_array, axis=1)\n307 | \n308 |         if self.contamination == \"auto\":\n309 |             # inliers score around -1 (the higher, the less abnormal).\n310 |             self.offset_ = -1.5\n311 |         else:\n312 |             self.offset_ = np.percentile(\n313 |                 self.negative_outlier_factor_, 100.0 * self.contamination\n314 |             )\n315 | \n316 |         return self\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression.fit`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n634 | def fit(self, X, Y):\n635 |         \"\"\"Fit model to data.\n636 | \n637 |         Parameters\n638 |         ----------\n639 |         X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n640 |             Training vectors, where `n_samples` is the number of samples and\n641 |             `n_features` is the number of predictors.\n642 | \n643 |         Y : array-like of shape (n_samples,) or (n_samples, n_targets)\n644 |             Target vectors, where `n_samples` is the number of samples and\n645 |             `n_targets` is the number of response variables.\n646 | \n647 |         Returns\n648 |         -------\n649 |         self : object\n650 |             Fitted model.\n651 |         \"\"\"\n652 |         super().fit(X, Y)\n653 |         # expose the fitted attributes `x_scores_` and `y_scores_`\n654 |         self.x_scores_ = self._x_scores\n655 |         self.y_scores_ = self._y_scores\n656 |         return self\n```\n\nFound file `sklearn/utils/validation.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `sklearn/utils/validation.py` for the full content if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"non_negative_factorize\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `sklearn/decomposition/__init__.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | The :mod:`sklearn.decomposition` module includes matrix decomposition\n 3 | algorithms, including among others PCA, NMF or ICA. Most of the algorithms of\n 4 | this module can be regarded as dimensionality reduction techniques.\n 5 | \"\"\"\n 6 | \n 7 | \n 8 | from ._nmf import NMF, non_negative_factorization\n 9 | from ._pca import PCA\n10 | from ._incremental_pca import IncrementalPCA\n11 | from ._kernel_pca import KernelPCA\n12 | from ._sparse_pca import SparsePCA, MiniBatchSparsePCA\n13 | from ._truncated_svd import TruncatedSVD\n14 | from ._fastica import FastICA, fastica\n15 | from ._dict_learning import (\n16 |     dict_learning,\n17 |     dict_learning_online,\n18 |     sparse_encode,\n19 |     DictionaryLearning,\n20 |     MiniBatchDictionaryLearning,\n21 |     SparseCoder,\n22 | )\n23 | from ._factor_analysis import FactorAnalysis\n24 | from ..utils.extmath import randomized_svd\n25 | from ._lda import LatentDirichletAllocation\n26 | \n27 | \n28 | __all__ = [\n29 |     \"DictionaryLearning\",\n30 |     \"FastICA\",\n31 |     \"IncrementalPCA\",\n32 |     \"KernelPCA\",\n33 |     \"MiniBatchDictionaryLearning\",\n34 |     \"MiniBatchSparsePCA\",\n35 |     \"NMF\",\n36 |     \"PCA\",\n37 |     \"SparseCoder\",\n38 |     \"SparsePCA\",\n39 |     \"dict_learning\",\n40 |     \"dict_learning_online\",\n41 |     \"fastica\",\n42 |     \"non_negative_factorization\",\n43 |     \"randomized_svd\",\n44 |     \"sparse_encode\",\n45 |     \"FactorAnalysis\",\n46 |     \"TruncatedSVD\",\n47 |     \"LatentDirichletAllocation\",\n48 | ]\n49 | \n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"non_negative_factorize\", \"NonNegativeMatrixFactorization non_negative_factorize\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:non_negative_factorization`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 874 | def non_negative_factorization(\n 875 |     X,\n 876 |     W=None,\n 877 |     H=None,\n 878 |     n_components=None,\n 879 |     *,\n 880 |     init=None,\n 881 |     update_H=True,\n 882 |     solver=\"cd\",\n 883 |     beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n 884 |     tol=1e-4,\n 885 |     max_iter=200,\n 886 |     alpha=\"deprecated\",\n 887 |     alpha_W=0.0,\n 888 |     alpha_H=\"same\",\n 889 |     l1_ratio=0.0,\n 890 |     regularization=\"deprecated\",\n 891 |     random_state=None,\n 892 |     verbose=0,\n 893 |     shuffle=False,\n 894 | ):\n 895 |     \"\"\"Compute Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF).\n 896 | \n 897 |     Find two non-negative matrices (W, H) whose product approximates the non-\n 898 |     negative matrix X. This factorization can be used for example for\n 899 |     dimensionality reduction, source separation or topic extraction.\n 900 | \n 901 |     The objective function is:\n 902 | \n 903 |         .. math::\n 904 | \n 905 |             0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n 906 | \n 907 |             + alpha\\\\_W * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n 908 | \n 909 |             + alpha\\\\_H * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n 910 | \n 911 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n 912 | \n 913 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n 914 | \n 915 |     Where:\n 916 | \n 917 |     :math:`||A||_{Fro}^2 = \\\\sum_{i,j} A_{ij}^2` (Frobenius norm)\n 918 | \n 919 |     :math:`||vec(A)||_1 = \\\\sum_{i,j} abs(A_{ij})` (Elementwise L1 norm)\n 920 | \n 921 |     The generic norm :math:`||X - WH||_{loss}^2` may represent\n 922 |     the Frobenius norm or another supported beta-divergence loss.\n 923 |     The choice between options is controlled by the `beta_loss` parameter.\n 924 | \n 925 |     The regularization terms are scaled by `n_features` for `W` and by `n_samples` for\n 926 |     `H` to keep their impact balanced with respect to one another and to the data fit\n 927 |     term as independent as possible of the size `n_samples` of the training set.\n 928 | \n 929 |     The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of W\n 930 |     and H. If H is given and update_H=False, it solves for W only.\n 931 | \n 932 |     Note that the transformed data is named W and the components matrix is named H. In\n 933 |     the NMF literature, the naming convention is usually the opposite since the data\n 934 |     matrix X is transposed.\n 935 | \n 936 |     Parameters\n 937 |     ----------\n 938 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 939 |         Constant matrix.\n 940 | \n 941 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components), default=None\n 942 |         If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n 943 | \n 944 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features), default=None\n 945 |         If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n 946 |         If update_H=False, it is used as a constant, to solve for W only.\n 947 | \n 948 |     n_components : int, default=None\n 949 |         Number of components, if n_components is not set all features\n 950 |         are kept.\n 951 | \n 952 |     init : {'random', 'nndsvd', 'nndsvda', 'nndsvdar', 'custom'}, default=None\n 953 |         Method used to initialize the procedure.\n 954 | \n 955 |         Valid options:\n 956 | \n 957 |         - None: 'nndsvda' if n_components < n_features, otherwise 'random'.\n 958 | \n 959 |         - 'random': non-negative random matrices, scaled with:\n 960 |             sqrt(X.mean() / n_components)\n 961 | \n 962 |         - 'nndsvd': Nonnegative Double Singular Value Decomposition (NNDSVD)\n 963 |             initialization (better for sparseness)\n 964 | \n 965 |         - 'nndsvda': NNDSVD with zeros filled with the average of X\n 966 |             (better when sparsity is not desired)\n 967 | \n 968 |         - 'nndsvdar': NNDSVD with zeros filled with small random values\n 969 |             (generally faster, less accurate alternative to NNDSVDa\n 970 |             for when sparsity is not desired)\n 971 | \n 972 |         - 'custom': use custom matrices W and H if `update_H=True`. If\n 973 |           `update_H=False`, then only custom matrix H is used.\n 974 | \n 975 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n 976 |             The default value of `init` changed from 'random' to None in 0.23.\n 977 | \n 978 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.1\n 979 |             When `init=None` and n_components is less than n_samples and n_features\n 980 |             defaults to `nndsvda` instead of `nndsvd`.\n 981 | \n 982 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n 983 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n 984 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n 985 | \n 986 |     solver : {'cd', 'mu'}, default='cd'\n 987 |         Numerical solver to use:\n 988 | \n 989 |         - 'cd' is a Coordinate Descent solver that uses Fast Hierarchical\n 990 |             Alternating Least Squares (Fast HALS).\n 991 | \n 992 |         - 'mu' is a Multiplicative Update solver.\n 993 | \n 994 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n 995 |            Coordinate Descent solver.\n 996 | \n 997 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n 998 |            Multiplicative Update solver.\n 999 | \n1000 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n1001 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n1002 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n1003 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n1004 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n1005 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n1006 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros. Used only in 'mu' solver.\n1007 | \n1008 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n1009 | \n1010 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n1011 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n1012 | \n1013 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n1014 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n1015 | \n1016 |     alpha : float, default=0.0\n1017 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms. Set it to zero to have no\n1018 |         regularization. When using `alpha` instead of `alpha_W` and `alpha_H`, the\n1019 |         regularization terms are not scaled by the `n_features` (resp. `n_samples`)\n1020 |         factors for `W` (resp. `H`).\n1021 | \n1022 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1023 |             The `alpha` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in 1.2.\n1024 |             Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1025 | \n1026 |     alpha_W : float, default=0.0\n1027 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `W`. Set it to zero\n1028 |         (default) to have no regularization on `W`.\n1029 | \n1030 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1031 | \n1032 |     alpha_H : float or \"same\", default=\"same\"\n1033 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `H`. Set it to zero to\n1034 |         have no regularization on `H`. If \"same\" (default), it takes the same value as\n1035 |         `alpha_W`.\n1036 | \n1037 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1038 | \n1039 |     l1_ratio : float, default=0.0\n1040 |         The regularization mixing parameter, with 0 <= l1_ratio <= 1.\n1041 |         For l1_ratio = 0 the penalty is an elementwise L2 penalty\n1042 |         (aka Frobenius Norm).\n1043 |         For l1_ratio = 1 it is an elementwise L1 penalty.\n1044 |         For 0 < l1_ratio < 1, the penalty is a combination of L1 and L2.\n1045 | \n1046 |     regularization : {'both', 'components', 'transformation'}, default=None\n1047 |         Select whether the regularization affects the components (H), the\n1048 |         transformation (W), both or none of them.\n1049 | \n1050 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1051 |             The `regularization` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in\n1052 |             1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1053 | \n1054 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1055 |         Used for NMF initialisation (when ``init`` == 'nndsvdar' or\n1056 |         'random'), and in Coordinate Descent. Pass an int for reproducible\n1057 |         results across multiple function calls.\n1058 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1059 | \n1060 |     verbose : int, default=0\n1061 |         The verbosity level.\n1062 | \n1063 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n1064 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n1065 | \n1066 |     Returns\n1067 |     -------\n1068 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1069 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1070 | \n1071 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1072 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1073 | \n1074 |     n_iter : int\n1075 |         Actual number of iterations.\n1076 | \n1077 |     Examples\n1078 |     --------\n1079 |     >>> import numpy as np\n1080 |     >>> X = np.array([[1,1], [2, 1], [3, 1.2], [4, 1], [5, 0.8], [6, 1]])\n1081 |     >>> from sklearn.decomposition import non_negative_factorization\n1082 |     >>> W, H, n_iter = non_negative_factorization(X, n_components=2,\n1083 |     ... init='random', random_state=0)\n1084 | \n1085 |     References\n1086 |     ----------\n1087 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n1088 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n1089 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n1090 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n1091 | \n1092 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n1093 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n1094 |     \"\"\"\n1095 |     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32])\n1096 | \n1097 |     est = NMF(\n1098 |         n_components=n_components,\n1099 |         init=init,\n1100 |         solver=solver,\n1101 |         beta_loss=beta_loss,\n1102 |         tol=tol,\n1103 |         max_iter=max_iter,\n1104 |         random_state=random_state,\n1105 |         alpha=alpha,\n1106 |         alpha_W=alpha_W,\n1107 |         alpha_H=alpha_H,\n1108 |         l1_ratio=l1_ratio,\n1109 |         verbose=verbose,\n1110 |         shuffle=shuffle,\n1111 |         regularization=regularization,\n1112 |     )\n1113 | \n1114 |     with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n1115 |         W, H, n_iter = est._fit_transform(X, W=W, H=H, update_H=update_H)\n1116 | \n1117 |     return W, H, n_iter\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"NonNegativeMatrixFactorization non_negative_factorize\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `sklearn/decomposition/__init__.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | The :mod:`sklearn.decomposition` module includes matrix decomposition\n 3 | algorithms, including among others PCA, NMF or ICA. Most of the algorithms of\n 4 | this module can be regarded as dimensionality reduction techniques.\n 5 | \"\"\"\n 6 | \n 7 | \n 8 | from ._nmf import NMF, non_negative_factorization\n 9 | from ._pca import PCA\n10 | from ._incremental_pca import IncrementalPCA\n11 | from ._kernel_pca import KernelPCA\n12 | from ._sparse_pca import SparsePCA, MiniBatchSparsePCA\n13 | from ._truncated_svd import TruncatedSVD\n14 | from ._fastica import FastICA, fastica\n15 | from ._dict_learning import (\n16 |     dict_learning,\n17 |     dict_learning_online,\n18 |     sparse_encode,\n19 |     DictionaryLearning,\n20 |     MiniBatchDictionaryLearning,\n21 |     SparseCoder,\n22 | )\n23 | from ._factor_analysis import FactorAnalysis\n24 | from ..utils.extmath import randomized_svd\n25 | from ._lda import LatentDirichletAllocation\n26 | \n27 | \n28 | __all__ = [\n29 |     \"DictionaryLearning\",\n30 |     \"FastICA\",\n31 |     \"IncrementalPCA\",\n32 |     \"KernelPCA\",\n33 |     \"MiniBatchDictionaryLearning\",\n34 |     \"MiniBatchSparsePCA\",\n35 |     \"NMF\",\n36 |     \"PCA\",\n37 |     \"SparseCoder\",\n38 