dation.\n1387 | \n1388 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1389 |         Training vector, where `n_samples` is the number of samples and\n1390 |         `n_features` is the number of features.\n1391 | \n1392 |     y : array-like of shape (n_samples,) or (n_samples, n_outputs)\n1393 |         Target relative to X for classification or regression;\n1394 |         None for unsupervised learning.\n1395 | \n1396 |     groups : array-like of  shape (n_samples,), default=None\n1397 |         Group labels for the samples used while splitting the dataset into\n1398 |         train/test set. Only used in conjunction with a \"Group\" :term:`cv`\n1399 |         instance (e.g., :class:`GroupKFold`).\n1400 | \n1401 |     train_sizes : array-like of shape (n_ticks,), \\\n1402 |             default=np.linspace(0.1, 1.0, 5)\n1403 |         Relative or absolute numbers of training examples that will be used to\n1404 |         generate the learning curve. If the dtype is float, it is regarded as a\n1405 |         fraction of the maximum size of the training set (that is determined\n1406 |         by the selected validation method), i.e. it has to be within (0, 1].\n1407 |         Otherwise it is interpreted as absolute sizes of the training sets.\n1408 |         Note that for classification the number of samples usually have to\n1409 |         be big enough to contain at least one sample from each class.\n1410 | \n1411 |     cv : int, cross-validation generator or an iterable, default=None\n1412 |         Determines the cross-validation splitting strategy.\n1413 |         Possible inputs for cv are:\n1414 | \n1415 |         - None, to use the default 5-fold cross validation,\n1416 |         - int, to specify the number of folds in a `(Stratified)KFold`,\n1417 |         - :term:`CV splitter`,\n1418 |         - An iterable yielding (train, test) splits as arrays of indices.\n1419 | \n1420 |         For int/None inputs, if the estimator is a classifier and ``y`` is\n1421 |         either binary or multiclass, :class:`StratifiedKFold` is used. In all\n1422 |         other cases, :class:`KFold` is used. These splitters are instantiated\n1423 |         with `shuffle=False` so the splits will be the same across calls.\n1424 | \n1425 |         Refer :ref:`User Guide <cross_validation>` for the various\n1426 |         cross-validation strategies that can be used here.\n1427 | \n1428 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.22\n1429 |             ``cv`` default value if None changed from 3-fold to 5-fold.\n1430 | \n1431 |     scoring : str or callable, default=None\n1432 |         A str (see model evaluation documentation) or\n1433 |         a scorer callable object / function with signature\n1434 |         ``scorer(estimator, X, y)``.\n1435 | \n1436 |     exploit_incremental_learning : bool, default=False\n1437 |         If the estimator supports incremental learning, this will be\n1438 |         used to speed up fitting for different training set sizes.\n1439 | \n1440 |     n_jobs : int, default=None\n1441 |         Number of jobs to run in parallel. Training the estimator and computing\n1442 |         the score are parallelized over the different training and test sets.\n1443 |         ``None`` means 1 unless in a :obj:`joblib.parallel_backend` context.\n1444 |         ``-1`` means using all processors. See :term:`Glossary <n_jobs>`\n1445 |         for more details.\n1446 | \n1447 |     pre_dispatch : int or str, default='all'\n1448 |         Number of predispatched jobs for parallel execution (default is\n1449 |         all). The option can reduce the allocated memory. The str can\n1450 |         be an expression like '2*n_jobs'.\n1451 | \n1452 |     verbose : int, default=0\n1453 |         Controls the verbosity: the higher, the more messages.\n1454 | \n1455 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n1456 |         Whether to shuffle training data before taking prefixes of it\n1457 |         based on``train_sizes``.\n1458 | \n1459 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1460 |         Used when ``shuffle`` is True. Pass an int for reproducible\n1461 |         output across multiple function calls.\n1462 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1463 | \n1464 |     error_score : 'raise' or numeric, default=np.nan\n1465 |         Value to assign to the score if an error occurs in estimator fitting.\n1466 |         If set to 'raise', the error is raised.\n1467 |         If a numeric value is given, FitFailedWarning is raised.\n1468 | \n1469 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n1470 | \n1471 |     return_times : bool, default=False\n1472 |         Whether to return the fit and score times.\n1473 | \n1474 |     fit_params : dict, default=None\n1475 |         Parameters to pass to the fit method of the estimator.\n1476 | \n1477 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n1478 | \n1479 |     Returns\n1480 |     -------\n1481 |     train_sizes_abs : array of shape (n_unique_ticks,)\n1482 |         Numbers of training examples that has been used to generate the\n1483 |         learning curve. Note that the number of ticks might be less\n1484 |         than n_ticks because duplicate entries will be removed.\n1485 | \n1486 |     train_scores : array of shape (n_ticks, n_cv_folds)\n1487 |         Scores on training sets.\n1488 | \n1489 |     test_scores : array of shape (n_ticks, n_cv_folds)\n1490 |         Scores on test set.\n1491 | \n1492 |     fit_times : array of shape (n_ticks, n_cv_folds)\n1493 |         Times spent for fitting in seconds. Only present if ``return_times``\n1494 |         is True.\n1495 | \n1496 |     score_times : array of shape (n_ticks, n_cv_folds)\n1497 |         Times spent for scoring in seconds. Only present if ``return_times``\n1498 |         is True.\n1499 | \n1500 |     Notes\n1501 |     -----\n1502 |     See :ref:`examples/model_selection/plot_learning_curve.py\n1503 |     <sphx_glr_auto_examples_model_selection_plot_learning_curve.py>`\n1504 |     \"\"\"\n1505 