 data written to the file it is necessary to save the changes.\n2180 |         Note that creating an `ExcelWriter` object with a file name that already\n2181 |         exists will result in the contents of the existing file being erased.\n2182 | \n2183 |         Parameters\n2184 |         ----------\n2185 |         excel_writer : path-like, file-like, or ExcelWriter object\n2186 |             File path or existing ExcelWriter.\n2187 |         sheet_name : str, default 'Sheet1'\n2188 |             Name of sheet which will contain DataFrame.\n2189 |         na_rep : str, default ''\n2190 |             Missing data representation.\n2191 |         float_format : str, optional\n2192 |             Format string for floating point numbers. For example\n2193 |             ``float_format=\"%.2f\"`` will format 0.1234 to 0.12.\n2194 |         columns : sequence or list of str, optional\n2195 |             Columns to write.\n2196 |         header : bool or list of str, default True\n2197 |             Write out the column names. If a list of string is given it is\n2198 |             assumed to be aliases for the column names.\n2199 |         index : bool, default True\n2200 |             Write row names (index).\n2201 |         index_label : str or sequence, optional\n2202 |             Column label for index column(s) if desired. If not specified, and\n2203 |             `header` and `index` are True, then the index names are used. A\n2204 |             sequence should be given if the DataFrame uses MultiIndex.\n2205 |         startrow : int, default 0\n2206 |             Upper left cell row to dump data frame.\n2207 |         startcol : int, default 0\n2208 |             Upper left cell column to dump data frame.\n2209 |         engine : str, optional\n2210 |             Write engine to use, 'openpyxl' or 'xlsxwriter'. You can also set this\n2211 |             via the options ``io.excel.xlsx.writer`` or\n2212 |             ``io.excel.xlsm.writer``.\n2213 | \n2214 |         merge_cells : bool, default True\n2215 |             Write MultiIndex and Hierarchical Rows as merged cells.\n2216 |         {encoding_parameter}\n2217 |         inf_rep : str, default 'inf'\n2218 |             Representation for infinity (there is no native representation for\n2219 |             infinity in Excel).\n2220 |         {verbose_parameter}\n2221 |         freeze_panes : tuple of int (length 2), optional\n2222 |             Specifies the one-based bottommost row and rightmost column that\n2223 |             is to be frozen.\n2224 |         {storage_options}\n2225 | \n2226 |             .. versionadded:: {storage_options_versionadded}\n2227 |         {extra_parameters}\n2228 |         See Also\n2229 |         --------\n2230 |         to_csv : Write DataFrame to a comma-separated values (csv) file.\n2231 |         ExcelWriter : Class for writing DataFrame objects into excel sheets.\n2232 |         read_excel : Read an Excel file into a pandas DataFrame.\n2233 |         read_csv : Read a comma-separated values (csv) file into DataFrame.\n2234 |         io.formats.style.Styler.to_excel : Add styles to Excel sheet.\n2235 | \n2236 |         Notes\n2237 |         -----\n2238 |         For compatibility with :meth:`~DataFrame.to_csv`,\n2239 |         to_excel serializes lists and dicts to strings before writing.\n2240 | \n2241 |         Once a workbook has been saved it is not possible to write further\n2242 |         data without rewriting the whole workbook.\n2243 | \n2244 |         pandas will check the number of rows, columns,\n2245 |         and cell character count does not exceed Excel's limitations.\n2246 |         All other limitations must be checked by the user.\n2247 | \n2248 |         Examples\n2249 |         --------\n2250 | \n2251 |         Create, write to and save a workbook:\n2252 | \n2253 |         >>> df1 = pd.DataFrame(\n2254 |         ...     [[\"a\", \"b\"], [\"c\", \"d\"]],\n2255 |         ...     index=[\"row 1\", \"row 2\"],\n2256 |      