y name `_get_local_path`.\n```\n43 | def _get_local_path(openml_path: str, data_home: str) -> str:\n44 |     return os.path.join(data_home, 'openml.org', openml_path + \".gz\")\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"validate_checksum\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `sklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"RCV1 dataset.\n  2 | \n  3 | The dataset page is available at\n  4 | \n  5 |     http://jmlr.csail.mit.edu/papers/volume5/lewis04a/\n  6 | \"\"\"\n  7 | \n  8 | # Author: Tom Dupre la Tour\n  9 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n 10 | \n 11 | import logging\n 12 | \n 13 | from os import remove, makedirs\n 14 | from os.path import dirname, exists, join\n 15 | from gzip import GzipFile\n 16 | \n 17 | import numpy as np\n 18 | import scipy.sparse as sp\n 19 | import joblib\n 20 | \n 21 | from . import get_data_home\n 22 | from ._base import _pkl_filepath\n 23 | from ._base import _fetch_remote\n 24 | from ._base import RemoteFileMetadata\n 25 | from ._svmlight_format_io import load_svmlight_files\n 26 | from ..utils import shuffle as shuffle_\n 27 | from ..utils import Bunch\n 28 | from ..utils.validation import _deprecate_positional_args\n 29 | \n 30 | \n 31 | # The original vectorized data can be found at:\n 32 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt0.dat.gz\n 33 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt1.dat.gz\n 34 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt2.dat.gz\n 35 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt3.dat.gz\n 36 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_train.dat.gz\n 37 | # while the original stemmed token files can be found\n 38 | # in the README, section B.12.i.:\n 39 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/lyrl2004_rcv1v2_README.htm\n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 40 | XY_METADATA = (\n 41 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 42 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976069',\n 43 |         checksum=('ed40f7e418d10484091b059703eeb95a'\n 44 |                   'e3199fe042891dcec4be6696b9968374'),\n 45 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt0.dat.gz'),\n 46 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 47 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976066',\n 48 |         checksum=('87700668ae45d45d5ca1ef6ae9bd81ab'\n 49 |                   '0f5ec88cc95dcef9ae7838f727a13aa6'),\n 50 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt1.dat.gz'),\n 51 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 52 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976063',\n 53 |         checksum=('48143ac703cbe33299f7ae9f4995db4'\n 54 |                   '9a258690f60e5debbff8995c34841c7f5'),\n 55 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt2.dat.gz'),\n 56 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 57 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976060',\n 58 |         checksum=('dfcb0d658311481523c6e6ca0c3f5a3'\n 59 |                   'e1d3d12cde5d7a8ce629a9006ec7dbb39'),\n 60 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt3.dat.gz'),\n 61 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 62 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976057',\n 63 |         checksum=('5468f656d0ba7a83afc7ad44841cf9a5'\n 64 |                   '3048a5c083eedc005dcdb5cc768924ae'),\n 65 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_train.dat.gz')\n 66 | )\n 67 | \n 68 | # The original data can be found at:\n 69 | # http://jmlr.csail.mit.edu/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a08-topic-qrels/rcv1-v2.topics.qrels.gz\n 70 | TOPICS_METADATA = RemoteFileMetadata(\n 71 |     url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976048',\n 72 |     checksum=('2a98e5e5d8b770bded93afc8930d882'\n 73 |               '99474317fe14181aee1466cc754d0d1c1'),\n 74 |     filename='rcv1v2.topics.qrels.gz')\n 75 | \n 76 | logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 80 | def fetch_rcv1(*, data_home=None, subset='all', download_if_missing=True,\n 81 |                random_state=None, shuffle=False, return_X_y=False):\n 82 |     \"\"\"Load the RCV1 multilabel dataset (classification).\n 83 | \n 84 |     Download it if necessary.\n 85 | \n 86 |     Version: RCV1-v2, vectors, full sets, topics multilabels.\n 87 | \n 88 |     =================   =====================\n 89 |     Classes                               103\n 90 |     Samples total                      804414\n 91 |     Dimensionality                      47236\n 92 |     Features            real, between 0 and 1\n 93 |     =================   =====================\n 94 | \n 95 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <rcv1_dataset>`.\n 96 | \n 97 |     .. versionadded:: 0.17\n 98 | \n 99 |     Parameters\n100 |     ----------\n101 |     data_home : str, default=None\n102 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the datasets. By default\n103 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n104 | \n105 |     subset : {'train', 'test', 'all'}, default='all'\n106 |         Select the dataset to load: 'train' for the training set\n107 |         (23149 samples), 'test' for the test set (781265 samples),\n108 |         'all' for both, with the training samples first if shuffle is False.\n109 |         This follows the official LYRL2004 chronological split.\n110 | \n111 |     download_if_missing : bool, default=True\n112 |         If False, raise a IOError if the data is not locally available\n113 |         instead of trying to download the data from the source site.\n114 | \n115 |     random_state : int or RandomState instance, default=None\n116 |         Determines random number generation for dataset shuffling. Pass an int\n117 |         for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n118 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n119 | \n120 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n121 |         Whether to shuffle dataset.\n122 | \n123 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n124 |         If True, returns ``(dataset.data, dataset.target)`` instead of a Bunch\n125 |         object. See below for more information about the `dataset.data` and\n126 |         `dataset.target` object.\n127 | \n128 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n129 | \n130 |     Returns\n131 |     -------\n132 |     dataset : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n133 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n134 | \n135 |         data : scipy csr array, dtype np.float64, shape (804414, 47236)\n136 |             The array has 0.16% of non zero values.\n137 |         target : scipy csr array, dtype np.uint8, shape (804414, 103)\n138 |             Each sample has a value of 1 in its categories, and 0 in others.\n139 |             The array has 3.15% of non zero values.\n140 |         sample_id : numpy array, dtype np.uint32, shape (804414,)\n141 |             Identification number of each sample, as ordered in dataset.data.\n142 |         target_names : numpy array, dtype object, length (103)\n143 |             Names of each target (RCV1 topics), as ordered in dataset.target.\n144 |         DESCR : string\n145 |             Description of the RCV1 dataset.\n146 | \n147 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n148 | \n149 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n150 |     \"\"\"\n151 |     N_SAMPLES = 804414\n152 |     N_FEATURES = 47236\n153 |     N_CATEGORIES = 103\n154 |     N_TRAIN = 23149\n155 | \n156 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n157 |     rcv1_dir = join(data_home, \"RCV1\")\n158 |     if download_if_missing:\n159 |         if not exists(rcv1_dir):\n160 |             makedirs(rcv1_dir)\n161 | \n162 |     samples_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"samples.pkl\")\n163 |     sample_id_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"sample_id.pkl\")\n164 |     sample_topics_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"sample_topics.pkl\")\n165 |     topics_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"topics_names.pkl\")\n166 | \n167 |     # load data (X) and sample_id\n168 |     if download_if_missing and (not exists(samples_path) or\n169 |                                 not exists(sample_id_path)):\n170 |         files = []\n171 |         for each in XY_METADATA:\n172 |             logger.info(\"Downloading %s\" % each.url)\n173 |             file_path = _fetch_remote(each, dirname=rcv1_dir)\n174 |             files.append(GzipFile(filename=file_path))\n175 | \n176 |         Xy = load_svmlight_files(files, n_features=N_FEATURES)\n177 | \n178 |         # Training data is before testing data\n179 |         X = sp.vstack([Xy[8], Xy[0], Xy[2], Xy[4], Xy[6]]).tocsr()\n180 |         sample_id = np.hstack((Xy[9], Xy[1], Xy[3], Xy[5], Xy[7]))\n181 |         sample_id = sample_id.astype(np.uint32, copy=False)\n182 | \n183 |         joblib.dump(X, samples_path, compress=9)\n184 |         joblib.dump(sample_id, sample_id_path, compress=9)\n185 | \n186 |         # delete archives\n187 |         for f in files:\n188 |             f.close()\n189 |             remove(f.name)\n190 |     else:\n191 |         X = joblib.load(samples_path)\n192 |         sample_id = joblib.load(sample_id_path)\n193 | \n194 |     # load target (y), categories, and sample_id_bis\n195 |     if download_if_missing and (not exists(sample_topics_path) or\n196 |                                 not exists(topics_path)):\n197 |         logger.info(\"Downloading %s\" % TOPICS_METADATA.url)\n198 |         topics_archive_path = _fetch_remote(TOPICS_METADATA,\n199 |                                             dirname=rcv1_dir)\n200 | \n201 |         # parse the target file\n202 |         n_cat = -1\n203 |         n_doc = -1\n204 |         doc_previous = -1\n205 |         y = np.zeros((N_SAMPLES, N_CATEGORIES), dtype=np.uint8)\n206 |         sample_id_bis = np.zeros(N_SAMPLES, dtype=np.int32)\n207 |         category_names = {}\n208 |         with GzipFile(filename=topics_archive_path, mode='rb') as f:\n209 |             for line in f:\n210 |                 line_components = line.decode(\"ascii\").split(\" \")\n211 |                 if len(line_components) == 3:\n212 |                     cat, doc, _ = line_components\n213 |                     if cat not in category_names:\n214 |                         n_cat += 1\n215 |                         category_names[cat] = n_cat\n216 | \n217 |                     doc = int(doc)\n218 |                     if doc != doc_previous:\n219 |                         doc_previous = doc\n220 |                         n_doc += 1\n221 |                         sample_id_bis[n_doc] = doc\n222 |                     y[n_doc, category_names[cat]] = 1\n223 | \n224 |         # delete archive\n225 |         remove(topics_archive_path)\n226 | \n227 |         # Samples in X are ordered with sample_id,\n228 |         # whereas in y, they are ordered with sample_id_bis.\n229 |         permutation = _find_permutation(sample_id_bis, sample_id)\n230 |         y = y[permutation, :]\n231 | \n232 |         # save category names in a list, with same order than y\n233 |         categories = np.empty(N_CATEGORIES, dtype=object)\n234 |         for k in category_names.keys():\n235 |             categories[category_names[k]] = k\n236 | \n237 |         # reorder categories in lexicographic order\n238 |         order = np.argsort(categories)\n239 |         categories = categories[order]\n240 |         y = sp.csr_matrix(y[:, order])\n241 | \n242 |         joblib.dump(y, sample_topics_path, compress=9)\n243 |         joblib.dump(categories, topics_path, compress=9)\n244 |     else:\n245 |         y = joblib.load(sample_topics_path)\n246 |         categories = joblib.load(topics_path)\n247 | \n248 |     if subset == 'all':\n249 |         pass\n250 |     elif subset == 'train':\n251 |         X = X[:N_TRAIN, :]\n252 |         y = y[:N_TRAIN, :]\n253 |         sample_id = sample_id[:N_TRAIN]\n254 |     elif subset == 'test':\n255 |         X = X[N_TRAIN:, :]\n256 |         y = y[N_TRAIN:, :]\n257 |         sample_id = sample_id[N_TRAIN:]\n258 |     else:\n259 |         raise ValueError(\"Unknown subset parameter. Got '%s' instead of one\"\n260 |                          \" of ('all', 'train', test')\" % subset)\n261 | \n262 |     if shuffle:\n263 |         X, y, sample_id = shuffle_(X, y, sample_id, random_state=random_state)\n264 | \n265 |     module_path = dirname(__file__)\n266 |     with open(join(module_path, 'descr', 'rcv1.rst')) as rst_file:\n267 |         fdescr = rst_file.read()\n268 | \n269 |     if return_X_y:\n270 |         return X, y\n271 | \n272 |     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, sample_id=sample_id,\n273 |                  target_names=categories, DESCR=fdescr)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/pipeline.py:Pipeline._validate_steps`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n143 | def _validate_steps(self):\n144 |         names, estimators = zip(*self.steps)\n145 | \n146 |         # validate names\n147 |         self._validate_names(names)\n148 | \n149 |         # validate estimators\n150 |         transformers = estimators[:-1]\n151 |         estimator = estimators[-1]\n152 | \n153 |         for t in transformers:\n154 |             if t is None or t == 'passthrough':\n155 |                 continue\n156 |             if (not (hasattr(t, \"fit\") or hasattr(t, \"fit_transform\")) or not\n157 |                     hasattr(t, \"transform\")):\n158 |                 raise TypeError(\"All intermediate steps should be \"\n159 |                                 \"transformers and implement fit and transform \"\n160 |                                 \"or be the string 'passthrough' \"\n161 |                                 \"'%s' (type %s) doesn't\" % (t, type(t)))\n162 | \n163 |         # We allow last estimator to be None as an identity transformation\n164 |         if (estimator is not None and estimator != 'passthrough'\n165 |                 and not hasattr(estimator, \"fit\")):\n166 |             raise TypeError(\n167 |                 \"Last step of Pipeline should implement fit \"\n168 |                 \"or be the string 'passthrough'. \"\n169 |                 \"'%s' (type %s) doesn't\" % (estimator, type(estimator)))\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/pipeline.py:FeatureUnion._validate_transformers`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n855 | def _validate_transformers(self):\n856 |         names, transformers = zip(*self.transformer_list)\n857 | \n858 |         # validate names\n859 |         self._validate_names(names)\n860 | \n861 |         # validate estimators\n862 |         for t in transformers:\n863 |             if t == 'drop':\n864 |                 continue\n865 |             if (not (hasattr(t, \"fit\") or hasattr(t, \"fit_transform\")) or not\n866 |                     hasattr(t, \"transform\")):\n867 |                 raise TypeError(\"All estimators should implement fit and \"\n868 |                                 \"transform. '%s' (type %s) doesn't\" %\n869 |                                 (t, type(t)))\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator._validate_data`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n379 | def _validate_data(self, X, y=None, reset=True,\n380 |                        validate_separately=False, **check_params):\n381 |         \"\"\"Validate input data and set or check the `n_features_in_` attribute.\n382 | \n383 |         Parameters\n384 |         ----------\n385 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix, dataframe} of shape \\\n386 |                 (n_samples, n_features)\n387 |             The input samples.\n388 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n389 |             The targets. If None, `check_array` is called on `X` and\n390 |             `check_X_y` is called otherwise.\n391 |         reset : bool, default=True\n392 |             Whether to reset the `n_features_in_` attribute.\n393 |             If False, the input will be checked for consistency with data\n394 |             provided when reset was last True.\n395 |         validate_separately : False or tuple of dicts, default=False\n396 |             Only used if y is not None.\n397 |             If False, call validate_X_y(). Else, it must be a tuple of kwargs\n398 |             to be used for calling check_array() on X and y respectively.\n399 |         **check_params : kwargs\n400 |             Parameters passed to :func:`sklearn.utils.check_array` or\n401 |             :func:`sklearn.utils.check_X_y`. Ignored if validate_separately\n402 |             is not False.\n403 | \n404 |         Returns\n405 |         -------\n406 |         out : {ndarray, sparse matrix} or tuple of these\n407 |             The validated input. A tuple is returned if `y` is not None.\n408 |         \"\"\"\n409 | \n410 |         if y is None:\n411 |             if self._get_tags()['requires_y']:\n412 |                 raise ValueError(\n413 |                     f\"This {self.__class__.__name__} estimator \"\n414 |                     f\"requires y to be passed, but the target y is None.\"\n415 |                 )\n416 |             X = check_array(X, **check_params)\n417 |             out = X\n418 |         else:\n419 |             if validate_separately:\n420 |                 # We need this because some estimators validate X and y\n421 |                 # separately, and in general, separately calling check_array()\n422 |                 # on X and y isn't equivalent to just calling check_X_y()\n423 |                 # :(\n424 |                 check_X_params, check_y_params = validate_separately\n425 |                 X = check_array(X, **check_X_params)\n426 |                 y = check_array(y, **check_y_params)\n427 |             else:\n428 |                 X, y = check_X_y(X, y, **check_params)\n429 |             out = X, y\n430 | \n431 |         if check_params.get('ensure_2d', True):\n432 |             self._check_n_features(X, reset=reset)\n433 | \n434 |         return out\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_fit_params`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1344 | def _check_fit_params(X, fit_params, indices=None):\n1345 |     \"\"\"Check and validate the parameters passed during `fit`.\n1346 | \n1347 |     Parameters\n1348 |     ----------\n1349 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1350 |         Data array.\n1351 | \n1352 |     fit_params : dict\n1353 |         Dictionary containing the parameters passed at fit.\n1354 | \n1355 |     indices : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n1356 |         Indices to be selected if the parameter has the same size as `X`.\n1357 | \n1358 |     Returns\n1359 |     -------\n1360 |     fit_params_validated : dict\n1361 |         Validated parameters. We ensure that the values support indexing.\n1362 |     \"\"\"\n1363 |     from . import _safe_indexing\n1364 |     fit_params_validated = {}\n1365 |     for param_key, param_value in fit_params.items():\n1366 |         if (not _is_arraylike(param_value) or\n1367 |                 _num_samples(param_value) != _num_samples(X)):\n1368 |             # Non-indexable pass-through (for now for backward-compatibility).\n1369 |             # https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/issues/15805\n1370 |             fit_params_validated[param_key] = param_value\n1371 |         else:\n1372 |             # Any other fit_params should support indexing\n1373 |             # (e.g. for cross-validation).\n1374 |             fit_params_validated[param_key] = _make_indexable(param_value)\n1375 |             fit_params_validated[param_key] = _safe_indexing(\n1376 |                 fit_params_validated[param_key], indices\n1377 |             )\n1378 | \n1379 |     return fit_params_validated\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check the ARFF-related files:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['sklearn/externals/_arff.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"sklearn/externals/_arff.py\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sklearn/externals/_arff.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/externals/_arff.py`.\n```\n   1 | # -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n   2 | # =============================================================================\n   3 | # Federal University of Rio Grande do Sul (UFRGS)\n   4 | # Connectionist Artificial Intelligence Laboratory (LIAC)\n   5 | # Renato de Pontes Pereira - rppereira@inf.ufrgs.br\n   6 | # =============================================================================\n   7 | # Copyright (c) 2011 Renato de Pontes Pereira, renato.ppontes at gmail dot com\n   8 | #\n   9 | # Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\n  10 | # of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\n  11 | # in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\n  12 | # to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\n  13 | # copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\n  14 | # furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n  15 | #\n  16 | # The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in\n  17 | # all copies or substantial portions of the Software.\n  18 | #\n  19 | # THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\n  20 | # IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\n  21 | # FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\n  22 | # AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\n  23 | # LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\n  24 | # OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\n  25 | # SOFTWARE.\n  26 | # =============================================================================\n  27 | \n  28 | '''\n  29 | The liac-arff module implements functions to read and write ARFF files in\n  30 | Python. It was created in the Connectionist Artificial Intelligence Laboratory\n  31 | (LIAC), which takes place at the Federal University of Rio Grande do Sul\n  32 | (UFRGS), in Brazil.\n  33 | \n  34 | ARFF (Attribute-Relation File Format) is an file format specially created for\n  35 | describe datasets which are commonly used for machine learning experiments and\n  36 | softwares. This file format was created to be used in Weka, the best\n  37 | representative software for machine learning automated experiments.\n  38 | \n  39 | An ARFF file can be divided into two sections: header and data. The Header\n  40 | describes the metadata of the dataset, including a general description of the\n  41 | dataset, its name and its attributes. The source below is an example of a\n  42 | header section in a XOR dataset::\n  43 | \n  44 |     %\n  45 |     % XOR Dataset\n  46 |     %\n  47 |     % Created by Renato Pereira\n  48 |     %            rppereira@inf.ufrgs.br\n  49 |     %            http://inf.ufrgs.br/~rppereira\n  50 |     %\n  51 |     %\n  52 |     @RELATION XOR\n  53 | \n  54 |     @ATTRIBUTE input1 REAL\n  55 |     @ATTRIBUTE input2 REAL\n  56 |     @ATTRIBUTE y REAL\n  57 | \n  58 | The Data section of an ARFF file describes the observations of the dataset, in\n  59 | the case of XOR dataset::\n  60 | \n  61 |     @DATA\n  62 |     0.0,0.0,0.0\n  63 |     0.0,1.0,1.0\n  64 |     1.0,0.0,1.0\n  65 |     1.0,1.0,0.0\n  66 |     %\n  67 |     %\n  68 |     %\n  69 | \n  70 | Notice that several lines are starting with an ``%`` symbol, denoting a\n  71 | comment, thus, lines with ``%`` at the beginning will be ignored, except by the\n  72 | description part at the beginning of the file. The declarations ``@RELATION``,\n  73 | ``@ATTRIBUTE``, and ``@DATA`` are all case insensitive and obligatory.\n  74 | \n  75 | For more information and details about the ARFF file description, consult\n  76 | http://www.cs.waikato.ac.nz/~ml/weka/arff.html\n  77 | \n  78 | \n  79 | ARFF Files in Python\n  80 | ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  81 | \n  82 | This module uses built-ins python objects to represent a deserialized ARFF\n  83 | file. A dictionary is used as the container of the data and metadata of ARFF,\n  84 | and have the following keys:\n  85 | \n  86 | - **description**: (OPTIONAL) a string with the description of the dataset.\n  87 | - **relation**: (OBLIGATORY) a string with the name of the dataset.\n  88 | - **attributes**: (OBLIGATORY) a list of attributes with the following\n  89 |   template::\n  90 | \n  91 |     (attribute_name, attribute_type)\n  92 | \n  93 |   the attribute_name is a string, and attribute_type must be an string\n  94 |   or a list of strings.\n  95 | - **data**: (OBLIGATORY) a list of data instances. Each data instance must be\n  96 |   a list with values, depending on the attributes.\n  97 | \n  98 | The above keys must follow the case which were described, i.e., the keys are\n  99 | case sensitive. The attribute type ``attribute_type`` must be one of these\n 100 | strings (they are not case sensitive): ``NUMERIC``, ``INTEGER``, ``REAL`` or\n 101 | ``STRING``. For nominal attributes, the ``attribute_type`` must be a list of\n 102 | strings.\n 103 | \n 104 | In this format, the XOR dataset presented above can be represented as a python\n 105 | object as::\n 106 | \n 107 |     xor_dataset = {\n 108 |         'description': 'XOR Dataset',\n 109 |         'relation': 'XOR',\n 110 |         'attributes': [\n 111 |             ('input1', 'REAL'),\n 112 |             ('input2', 'REAL'),\n 113 |             ('y', 'REAL'),\n 114 |         ],\n 115 |         'data': [\n 116 |             [0.0, 0.0, 0.0],\n 117 |             [0.0, 1.0, 1.0],\n 118 |             [1.0, 0.0, 1.0],\n 119 |             [1.0, 1.0, 0.0]\n 120 |         ]\n 121 |     }\n 122 | \n 123 | \n 124 | Features\n 125 | ~~~~~~~~\n 126 | \n 127 | This module provides several features, including:\n 128 | \n 129 | - Read and write ARFF files using python built-in structures, such dictionaries\n 130 |   and lists;\n 131 | - Supports `scipy.sparse.coo <http://docs.scipy\n 132 |   .org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.sparse.coo_matrix.html#scipy.sparse.coo_matrix>`_\n 133 |   and lists of dictionaries as used by SVMLight\n 134 | - Supports the following attribute types: NUMERIC, REAL, INTEGER, STRING, and\n 135 |   NOMINAL;\n 136 | - Has an interface similar to other built-in modules such as ``json``, or\n 137 |   ``zipfile``;\n 138 | - Supports read and write the descriptions of files;\n 139 | - Supports missing values and names with spaces;\n 140 | - Supports unicode values and names;\n 141 | - Fully compatible with Python 2.7+, Python 3.3+, pypy and pypy3;\n 142 | - Under `MIT License <http://opensource.org/licenses/MIT>`_\n 143 | \n 144 | '''\n 145 | __author__ = 'Renato de Pontes Pereira, Matthias Feurer, Joel Nothman'\n 146 | __author_email__ = ('renato.ppontes@gmail.com, '\n 147 |                     'feurerm@informatik.uni-freiburg.de, '\n 148 |                     'joel.nothman@gmail.com')\n 149 | __version__ = '2.4.0'\n 150 | \n 151 | import typing\n 152 | from typing import Optional, List, Dict, Any, Iterator, Union, Tuple\n 153 | \n 154 | import re\n 155 | import sys\n 156 | import csv\n 157 | \n 158 | # CONSTANTS ===================================================================\n 159 | _SIMPLE_TYPES = ['NUMERIC', 'REAL', 'INTEGER', 'STRING']\n 160 | \n 161 | _TK_DESCRIPTION = '%'\n 162 | _TK_COMMENT     = '%'\n 163 | _TK_RELATION    = '@RELATION'\n 164 | _TK_ATTRIBUTE   = '@ATTRIBUTE'\n 165 | _TK_DATA        = '@DATA'\n 166 | \n 167 | _RE_RELATION     = re.compile(r'^([^\\{\\}%,\\s]*|\\\".*\\\"|\\'.*\\')$', re.UNICODE)\n 168 | _RE_ATTRIBUTE    = re.compile(r'^(\\\".*\\\"|\\'.*\\'|[^\\{\\}%,\\s]*)\\s+(.+)$', re.UNICODE)\n 169 | _RE_TYPE_NOMINAL = re.compile(r'^\\{\\s*((\\\".*\\\"|\\'.*\\'|\\S*)\\s*,\\s*)*(\\\".*\\\"|\\'.*\\'|\\S*)\\s*\\}$', re.UNICODE)\n 170 | _RE_QUOTE_CHARS = re.compile(r'[\"\\'\\\\\\s%,\\000-\\031]', re.UNICODE)\n 171 | _RE_ESCAPE_CHARS = re.compile(r'(?=[\"\\'\\\\%])|[\\n\\r\\t\\000-\\031]')\n 172 | _RE_SPARSE_LINE = re.compile(r'^\\s*\\{.*\\}\\s*$', re.UNICODE)\n 173 | _RE_NONTRIVIAL_DATA = re.compile('[\"\\'{}\\\\s]', re.UNICODE)\n 174 | \n 175 | ArffDenseDataType = Iterator[List]\n 176 | ArffSparseDataType = Tuple[List, ...]\n 177 | \n 178 | \n 179 | if typing.TYPE_CHECKING:\n 180 |     # typing_extensions is available when mypy is installed\n 181 |     from typing_extensions import TypedDict\n 182 | \n 183 |     class ArffContainerType(TypedDict):\n 184 |         description: str\n 185 |         relation: str\n 186 |         attributes: List\n 187 |         data: Union[ArffDenseDataType, ArffSparseDataType]\n 188 | \n 189 | else:\n 190 |     ArffContainerType = Dict[str, Any]\n 191 | \n 192 | \n 193 | def _build_re_values():\n 194 |     quoted_re = r'''\n 195 |                     \"      # open quote followed by zero or more of:\n 196 |                     (?:\n 197 |                         (?<!\\\\)    # no additional backslash\n 198 |                         (?:\\\\\\\\)*  # maybe escaped backslashes\n 199 |                         \\\\\"        # escaped quote\n 200 |                     |\n 201 |                         \\\\[^\"]     # escaping a non-quote\n 202 |                     |\n 203 |                         [^\"\\\\]     # non-quote char\n 204 |                     )*\n 205 |                     \"      # close quote\n 206 |                     '''\n 207 |     # a value is surrounded by \" or by ' or contains no quotables\n 208 |     value_re = r'''(?:\n 209 |         %s|          # a value may be surrounded by \"\n 210 |         %s|          # or by '\n 211 |         [^,\\s\"'{}]+  # or may contain no characters requiring quoting\n 212 |         )''' % (quoted_re,\n 213 |                 quoted_re.replace('\"', \"'\"))\n 214 | \n 215 |     # This captures (value, error) groups. Because empty values are allowed,\n 216 |     # we cannot just look for empty values to handle syntax errors.\n 217 |     # We presume the line has had ',' prepended...\n 218 |     dense = re.compile(r'''(?x)\n 219 |         ,                # may follow ','\n 220 |         \\s*\n 221 |         ((?=,)|$|%(value_re)s)  # empty or value\n 222 |         |\n 223 |         (\\S.*)           # error\n 224 |         ''' % {'value_re': value_re})\n 225 | \n 226 |     # This captures (key, value) groups and will have an empty key/value\n 227 |     # in case of syntax errors.\n 228 |     # It does not ensure that the line starts with '{' or ends with '}'.\n 229 |     sparse = re.compile(r'''(?x)\n 230 |         (?:^\\s*\\{|,)   # may follow ',', or '{' at line start\n 231 |         \\s*\n 232 |         (\\d+)          # attribute key\n 233 |         \\s+\n 234 |         (%(value_re)s) # value\n 235 |         |\n 236 |         (?!}\\s*$)      # not an error if it's }$\n 237 |         (?!^\\s*{\\s*}\\s*$)  # not an error if it's ^{}$\n 238 |         \\S.*           # error\n 239 |         ''' % {'value_re': value_re})\n 240 |     return dense, sparse\n 241 | \n 242 | \n 243 | _RE_DENSE_VALUES, _RE_SPARSE_KEY_VALUES = _build_re_values()\n 244 | \n 245 | \n 246 | _ESCAPE_SUB_MAP = {\n 247 |     '\\\\\\\\': '\\\\',\n 248 |     '\\\\\"': '\"',\n 249 |     \"\\\\'\": \"'\",\n 250 |     '\\\\t': '\\t',\n 251 |     '\\\\n': '\\n',\n 252 |     '\\\\r': '\\r',\n 253 |     '\\\\b': '\\b',\n 254 |     '\\\\f': '\\f',\n 255 |     '\\\\%': '%',\n 256 | }\n 257 | _UNESCAPE_SUB_MAP = {chr(i): '\\\\%03o' % i for i in range(32)}\n 258 | _UNESCAPE_SUB_MAP.update({v: k for k, v in _ESCAPE_SUB_MAP.items()})\n 259 | _UNESCAPE_SUB_MAP[''] = '\\\\'\n 260 | _ESCAPE_SUB_MAP.update({'\\\\%d' % i: chr(i) for i in range(10)})\n 261 | \n 262 | \n 263 | def _escape_sub_callback(match):\n 264 |     s = match.group()\n 265 |     if len(s) == 2:\n 266 |         try:\n 267 |             return _ESCAPE_SUB_MAP[s]\n 268 |         except KeyError:\n 269 |             raise ValueError('Unsupported escape sequence: %s' % s)\n 270 |     if s[1] == 'u':\n 271 |         return unichr(int(s[2:], 16))\n 272 |     else:\n 273 |         return chr(int(s[1:], 8))\n 274 | \n 275 | \n 276 | def _unquote(v):\n 277 |     if v[:1] in ('\"', \"'\"):\n 278 |         return re.sub(r'\\\\([0-9]{1,3}|u[0-9a-f]{4}|.)', _escape_sub_callback,\n 279 |                       v[1:-1])\n 280 |     elif v in ('?', ''):\n 281 |         return None\n 282 |     else:\n 283 |         return v\n 284 | \n 285 | \n 286 | def _parse_values(s):\n 287 |     '''(INTERNAL) Split a line into a list of values'''\n 288 |     if not _RE_NONTRIVIAL_DATA.search(s):\n 289 |         # Fast path for trivial cases (unfortunately we have to handle missing\n 290 |         # values because of the empty string case :(.)\n 291 |         return [None if s in ('?', '') else s\n 292 |                 for s in next(csv.reader([s]))]\n 293 | \n 294 |     # _RE_DENSE_VALUES tokenizes despite quoting, whitespace, etc.\n 295 |     values, errors = zip(*_RE_DENSE_VALUES.findall(',' + s))\n 296 |     if not any(errors):\n 297 |         return [_unquote(v) for v in values]\n 298 |     if _RE_SPARSE_LINE.match(s):\n 299 |         try:\n 300 |             return {int(k): _unquote(v)\n 301 |                     for k, v in _RE_SPARSE_KEY_VALUES.findall(s)}\n 302 |         except ValueError as exc:\n 303 |             # an ARFF syntax error in sparse data\n 304 |             for match in _RE_SPARSE_KEY_VALUES.finditer(s):\n 305 |                 if not match.group(1):\n 306 |                     raise BadLayout('Error parsing %r' % match.group())\n 307 |             raise BadLayout('Unknown parsing error')\n 308 |     else:\n 309 |         # an ARFF syntax error\n 310 |         for match in _RE_DENSE_VALUES.finditer(s):\n 311 |             if match.group(2):\n 312 |                 raise BadLayout('Error parsing %r' % match.group())\n 313 |         raise BadLayout('Unknown parsing error')\n 314 | \n 315 | \n 316 | DENSE = 0     # Constant value representing a dense matrix\n 317 | COO = 1       # Constant value representing a sparse matrix in coordinate format\n 318 | LOD = 2       # Constant value representing a sparse matrix in list of\n 319 |               # dictionaries format\n 320 | DENSE_GEN = 3 # Generator of dictionaries\n 321 | LOD_GEN = 4   # Generator of dictionaries\n 322 | _SUPPORTED_DATA_STRUCTURES = [DENSE, COO, LOD, DENSE_GEN, LOD_GEN]\n 323 | \n 324 | # =============================================================================\n 325 | \n 326 | # COMPATIBILITY WITH PYTHON 3 =================================================\n 327 | PY3 = sys.version_info[0] == 3\n 328 | if PY3:\n 329 |     unicode = str\n 330 |     basestring = str\n 331 |     xrange = range\n 332 |     unichr = chr\n 333 | # COMPABILITY WITH PYTHON 2 ===================================================\n 334 | # =============================================================================\n 335 | PY2 = sys.version_info[0] == 2\n 336 | if PY2:\n 337 |     from itertools import izip as zip\n 338 | \n 339 | # EXCEPTIONS ==================================================================\n 340 | class ArffException(Exception):\n 341 |     message : Optional[str] = None\n 342 | \n 343 |     def __init__(self):\n 344 |         self.line = -1\n 345 | \n 346 |     def __str__(self):\n 347 |         return self.message%self.line\n 348 | \n 349 | class BadRelationFormat(ArffException):\n 350 |     '''Error raised when the relation declaration is in an invalid format.'''\n 351 |     message = 'Bad @RELATION format, at line %d.'\n 352 | \n 353 | class BadAttributeFormat(ArffException):\n 354 |     '''Error raised when some attribute declaration is in an invalid format.'''\n 355 |     message = 'Bad @ATTRIBUTE format, at line %d.'\n 356 | \n 357 | class BadDataFormat(ArffException):\n 358 |     '''Error raised when some data instance is in an invalid format.'''\n 359 |     def __init__(self, value):\n 360 |         super(BadDataFormat, self).__init__()\n 361 |         self.message = (\n 362 |             'Bad @DATA instance format in line %d: ' +\n 363 |             ('%s' % value)\n 364 |         )\n 365 | \n 366 | class BadAttributeType(ArffException):\n 367 |     '''Error raised when some invalid type is provided into the attribute\n 368 |     declaration.'''\n 369 |     message = 'Bad @ATTRIBUTE type, at line %d.'\n 370 | \n 371 | class BadAttributeName(ArffException):\n 372 |     '''Error raised when an attribute name is provided twice the attribute\n 373 |     declaration.'''\n 374 | \n 375 |     def __init__(self, value, value2):\n 376 |         super(BadAttributeName, self).__init__()\n 377 |         self.message = (\n 378 |             ('Bad @ATTRIBUTE name %s at line' % value) +\n 379 |             ' %d, this name is already in use in line' +\n 380 |             (' %d.' % value2)\n 381 |         )\n 382 | \n 383 | class BadNominalValue(ArffException):\n 384 |     '''Error raised when a value in used in some data instance but is not\n 385 |     declared into it respective attribute declaration.'''\n 386 | \n 387 |     def __init__(self, value):\n 388 |         super(BadNominalValue, self).__init__()\n 389 |         self.message = (\n 390 |             ('Data value %s not found in nominal declaration, ' % value)\n 391 |             + 'at line %d.'\n 392 |         )\n 393 | \n 394 | class BadNominalFormatting(ArffException):\n 395 |     '''Error raised when a nominal value with space is not properly quoted.'''\n 396 |     def __init__(self, value):\n 397 |         super(BadNominalFormatting, self).__init__()\n 398 |         self.message = (\n 399 |             ('Nominal data value \"%s\" not properly quoted in line ' % value) +\n 400 |             '%d.'\n 401 |         )\n 402 | \n 403 | class BadNumericalValue(ArffException):\n 404 |     '''Error raised when and invalid numerical value is used in some data\n 405 |     instance.'''\n 406 |     message = 'Invalid numerical value, at line %d.'\n 407 | \n 408 | class BadStringValue(ArffException):\n 409 |     '''Error raise when a string contains space but is not quoted.'''\n 410 |     message = 'Invalid string value at line %d.'\n 411 | \n 412 | class BadLayout(ArffException):\n 413 |     '''Error raised when the layout of the ARFF file has something wrong.'''\n 414 |     message = 'Invalid layout of the ARFF file, at line %d.'\n 415 | \n 416 |     def __init__(self, msg=''):\n 417 |         super(BadLayout, self).__init__()\n 418 |         if msg:\n 419 |             self.message = BadLayout.message + ' ' + msg.replace('%', '%%')\n 420 | \n 421 | \n 422 | class BadObject(ArffException):\n 423 |     '''Error raised when the object representing the ARFF file has something\n 424 |     wrong.'''\n 425 |     def __init__(self, msg='Invalid object.'):\n 426 |         self.msg = msg\n 427 | \n 428 |     def __str__(self):\n 429 |         return '%s' % self.msg\n 430 | \n 431 | # =============================================================================\n 432 | \n 433 | # INTERNAL ====================================================================\n 434 | def _unescape_sub_callback(match):\n 435 |     return _UNESCAPE_SUB_MAP[match.group()]\n 436 | \n 437 | \n 438 | def encode_string(s):\n 439 |     if _RE_QUOTE_CHARS.search(s):\n 440 |         return u\"'%s'\" % _RE_ESCAPE_CHARS.sub(_unescape_sub_callback, s)\n 441 |     return s\n 442 | \n 443 | \n 444 | class EncodedNominalConversor(object):\n 445 |     def __init__(self, values):\n 446 |         self.values = {v: i for i, v in enumerate(values)}\n 447 |         self.values[0] = 0\n 448 | \n 449 |     def __call__(self, value):\n 450 |         try:\n 451 |             return self.values[value]\n 452 |         except KeyError:\n 453 |             raise BadNominalValue(value)\n 454 | \n 455 | \n 456 | class NominalConversor(object):\n 457 |     def __init__(self, values):\n 458 |         self.values = set(values)\n 459 |         self.zero_value = values[0]\n 460 | \n 461 |     def __call__(self, value):\n 462 |         if value not in self.values:\n 463 |             if value == 0:\n 464 |                 # Sparse decode\n 465 |                 # See issue #52: nominals should take their first value when\n 466 |                 # unspecified in a sparse matrix. Naturally, this is consistent\n 467 |                 # with EncodedNominalConversor.\n 468 |                 return self.zero_value\n 469 |             raise BadNominalValue(value)\n 470 |         return unicode(value)\n 471 | \n 472 | \n 473 | class DenseGeneratorData(object):\n 474 |     '''Internal helper class to allow for different matrix types without\n 475 |     making the code a huge collection of if statements.'''\n 476 | \n 477 |     def decode_rows(self, stream, conversors):\n 478 |         for row in stream:\n 479 |             values = _parse_values(row)\n 480 | \n 481 |             if isinstance(values, dict):\n 482 |                 if values and max(values) >= len(conversors):\n 483 |                     raise BadDataFormat(row)\n 484 |                 # XXX: int 0 is used for implicit values, not '0'\n 485 |                 values = [values[i] if i in values else 0 for i in\n 486 |                           xrange(len(conversors))]\n 487 |             else:\n 488 |                 if len(values) != len(conversors):\n 489 |                     raise BadDataFormat(row)\n 490 | \n 491 |             yield self._decode_values(values, conversors)\n 492 | \n 493 |     @staticmethod\n 494 |     def _decode_values(values, conversors):\n 495 |         try:\n 496 |             values = [None if value is None else conversor(value)\n 497 |                       for conversor, value\n 498 |                       in zip(conversors, values)]\n 499 |         except ValueError as exc:\n 500 |             if 'float: ' in str(exc):\n 501 |                 raise BadNumericalValue()\n 502 |         return values\n 503 | \n 504 |     def encode_data(self, data, attributes):\n 505 |         '''(INTERNAL) Encodes a line of data.\n 506 | \n 507 |         Data instances follow the csv format, i.e, attribute values are\n 508 |         delimited by commas. After converted from csv.\n 509 | \n 510 |         :param data: a list of values.\n 511 |         :param attributes: a list of attributes. Used to check if data is valid.\n 512 |         :return: a string with the encoded data line.\n 513 |         '''\n 514 |         current_row = 0\n 515 | \n 516 |         for inst in data:\n 517 |             if len(inst) != len(attributes):\n 518 |                 raise BadObject(\n 519 |                     'Instance %d has %d attributes, expected %d' %\n 520 |                      (current_row, len(inst), len(attributes))\n 521 |                 )\n 522 | \n 523 |             new_data = []\n 524 |             for value in inst:\n 525 |                 if value is None or value == u'' or value != value:\n 526 |                     s = '?'\n 527 |                 else:\n 528 |                     s = encode_string(unicode(value))\n 529 |                 new_data.append(s)\n 530 | \n 531 |             current_row += 1\n 532 |             yield u','.join(new_data)\n 533 | \n 534 | \n 535 | class _DataListMixin(object):\n 536 |     \"\"\"Mixin to return a list from decode_rows instead of a generator\"\"\"\n 537 |     def decode_rows(self, stream, conversors):\n 538 |         return list(super(_DataListMixin, self).decode_rows(stream, conversors))\n 539 | \n 540 | \n 541 | class Data(_DataListMixin, DenseGeneratorData):\n 542 |     pass\n 543 | \n 544 | \n 545 | class COOData(object):\n 546 |     def decode_rows(self, stream, conversors):\n 547 |         data, rows, cols = [], [], []\n 548 |         for i, row in enumerate(stream):\n 549 |             values = _parse_values(row)\n 550 |             if not isinstance(values, dict):\n 551 |                 raise BadLayout()\n 552 |             if not values:\n 553 |                 continue\n 554 |             row_cols, values = zip(*sorted(values.items()))\n 555 |             try:\n 556 |                 values = [value if value is None else conversors[key](value)\n 557 |                           for key, value in zip(row_cols, values)]\n 558 |             except ValueError as exc:\n 559 |                 if 'float: ' in str(exc):\n 560 |                     raise BadNumericalValue()\n 561 |                 raise\n 562 |             except IndexError:\n 563 |                 # conversor out of range\n 564 |                 raise BadDataFormat(row)\n 565 | \n 566 |             data.extend(values)\n 567 |             rows.extend([i] * len(values))\n 568 |             cols.extend(row_cols)\n 569 | \n 570 |         return data, rows, cols\n 571 | \n 572 |     def encode_data(self, data, attributes):\n 573 |         num_attributes = len(attributes)\n 574 |         new_data = []\n 575 |         current_row = 0\n 576 | \n 577 |         row = data.row\n 578 |         col = data.col\n 579 |         data = data.data\n 580 | \n 581 |         # Check if the rows are sorted\n 582 |         if not all(row[i] <= row[i + 1] for i in xrange(len(row) - 1)):\n 583 |             raise ValueError(\"liac-arff can only output COO matrices with \"\n 584 |                              \"sorted rows.\")\n 585 | \n 586 |         for v, col, row in zip(data, col, row):\n 587 |             if row > current_row:\n 588 |                 # Add empty rows if necessary\n 589 |                 while current_row < row:\n 590 |                     yield \" \".join([u\"{\", u','.join(new_data), u\"}\"])\n 591 |                     new_data = []\n 592 |                     current_row += 1\n 593 | \n 594 |             if col >= num_attributes:\n 595 |                 raise BadObject(\n 596 |                     'Instance %d has at least %d attributes, expected %d' %\n 597 |                     (current_row, col + 1, num_attributes)\n 598 |                 )\n 599 | \n 600 |             if v is None or v == u'' or v != v:\n 601 |                 s = '?'\n 602 |             else:\n 603 |                 s = encode_string(unicode(v))\n 604 |             new_data.append(\"%d %s\" % (col, s))\n 605 | \n 606 |         yield \" \".join([u\"{\", u','.join(new_data), u\"}\"])\n 607 | \n 608 | class LODGeneratorData(object):\n 609 |     def decode_rows(self, stream, conversors):\n 610 |         for row in stream:\n 611 |             values = _parse_values(row)\n 612 | \n 613 |             if not isinstance(values, dict):\n 614 |                 raise BadLayout()\n 615 |             try:\n 616 |                 yield {key: None if value is None else conversors[key](value)\n 617 |                        for key, value in values.items()}\n 618 |             except ValueError as exc:\n 619 |                 if 'float: ' in str(exc):\n 620 |                     raise BadNumericalValue()\n 621 |                 raise\n 622 |             except IndexError:\n 623 |                 # conversor out of range\n 624 |                 raise BadDataFormat(row)\n 625 | \n 626 |     def encode_data(self, data, attributes):\n 627 |         current_row = 0\n 628 | \n 629 |         num_attributes = len(attributes)\n 630 |         for row in data:\n 631 |             new_data = []\n 632 | \n 633 |             if len(row) > 0 and max(row) >= num_attributes:\n 634 |                 raise BadObject(\n 635 |                     'Instance %d has %d attributes, expected %d' %\n 636 |                     (current_row, max(row) + 1, num_attributes)\n 637 |                 )\n 638 | \n 639 |             for col in sorted(row):\n 640 |                 v = row[col]\n 641 |                 if v is None or v == u'' or v != v:\n 642 |                     s = '?'\n 643 |                 else:\n 644 |                     s = encode_string(unicode(v))\n 645 |                 new_data.append(\"%d %s\" % (col, s))\n 646 | \n 647 |             current_row += 1\n 648 |             yield \" \".join([u\"{\", u','.join(new_data), u\"}\"])\n 649 | \n 650 | class LODData(_DataListMixin, LODGeneratorData):\n 651 |     pass\n 652 | \n 653 | \n 654 | def _get_data_object_for_decoding(matrix_type):\n 655 |     if matrix_type == DENSE:\n 656 |         return Data()\n 657 |     elif matrix_type == COO:\n 658 |         return COOData()\n 659 |     elif matrix_type == LOD:\n 660 |         return LODData()\n 661 |     elif matrix_type == DENSE_GEN:\n 662 |         return DenseGeneratorData()\n 663 |     elif matrix_type == LOD_GEN:\n 664 |         return LODGeneratorData()\n 665 |     else:\n 666 |         raise ValueError(\"Matrix type %s not supported.\" % str(matrix_type))\n 667 | \n 668 | def _get_data_object_for_encoding(matrix):\n 669 |     # Probably a scipy.sparse\n 670 |     if hasattr(matrix, 'format'):\n 671 |         if matrix.format == 'coo':\n 672 |             return COOData()\n 673 |         else:\n 674 |             raise ValueError('Cannot guess matrix format!')\n 675 |     elif isinstance(matrix[0], dict):\n 676 |         return LODData()\n 677 |     else:\n 678 |         return Data()\n 679 | \n 680 | # =============================================================================\n 681 | \n 682 | # ADVANCED INTERFACE ==========================================================\n 683 | class ArffDecoder(object):\n 684 |     '''An ARFF decoder.'''\n 685 | \n 686 |     def __init__(self):\n 687 |         '''Constructor.'''\n 688 |         self._conversors = []\n 689 |         self._current_line = 0\n 690 | \n 691 |     def _decode_comment(self, s):\n 692 |         '''(INTERNAL) Decodes a comment line.\n 693 | \n 694 |         Comments are single line strings starting, obligatorily, with the ``%``\n 695 |         character, and can have any symbol, including whitespaces or special\n 696 |         characters.\n 697 | \n 698 |         This method must receive a normalized string, i.e., a string without\n 699 |         padding, including the \"\\r\\n\" characters.\n 700 | \n 701 |         :param s: a normalized string.\n 702 |         :return: a string with the decoded comment.\n 703 |         '''\n 704 |         res = re.sub(r'^\\%( )?', '', s)\n 705 |         return res\n 706 | \n 707 |     def _decode_relation(self, s):\n 708 |         '''(INTERNAL) Decodes a relation line.\n 709 | \n 710 |         The relation declaration is a line with the format ``@RELATION\n 711 |         <relation-name>``, where ``relation-name`` is a string. The string must\n 712 |         start with alphabetic character and must be quoted if the name includes\n 713 |         spaces, otherwise this method will raise a `BadRelationFormat` exception.\n 714 | \n 715 |         This method must receive a normalized string, i.e., a string without\n 716 |         padding, including the \"\\r\\n\" characters.\n 717 | \n 718 |         :param s: a normalized string.\n 719 |         :return: a string with the decoded relation name.\n 720 |         '''\n 721 |         _, v = s.split(' ', 1)\n 722 |         v = v.strip()\n 723 | \n 724 |         if not _RE_RELATION.match(v):\n 725 |             raise BadRelationFormat()\n 726 | \n 727 |         res = unicode(v.strip('\"\\''))\n 728 |         return res\n 729 | \n 730 |     def _decode_attribute(self, s):\n 731 |         '''(INTERNAL) Decodes an attribute line.\n 732 | \n 733 |         The attribute is the most complex declaration in an arff file. All\n 734 |         attributes must follow the template::\n 735 | \n 736 |              @attribute <attribute-name> <datatype>\n 737 | \n 738 |         where ``attribute-name`` is a string, quoted if the name contains any\n 739 |         whitespace, and ``datatype`` can be:\n 740 | \n 741 |         - Numerical attributes as ``NUMERIC``, ``INTEGER`` or ``REAL``.\n 742 |         - Strings as ``STRING``.\n 743 |         - Dates (NOT IMPLEMENTED).\n 744 |         - Nominal attributes with format:\n 745 | \n 746 |             {<nominal-name1>, <nominal-name2>, <nominal-name3>, ...}\n 747 | \n 748 |         The nominal names follow the rules for the attribute names, i.e., they\n 749 |         must be quoted if the name contains whitespaces.\n 750 | \n 751 |         This method must receive a normalized string, i.e., a string without\n 752 |         padding, including the \"\\r\\n\" characters.\n 753 | \n 754 |         :param s: a normalized string.\n 755 |         :return: a tuple (ATTRIBUTE_NAME, TYPE_OR_VALUES).\n 756 |         '''\n 757 |         _, v = s.split(' ', 1)\n 758 |         v = v.strip()\n 759 | \n 760 |         # Verify the general structure of declaration\n 761 |         m = _RE_ATTRIBUTE.match(v)\n 762 |         if not m:\n 763 |             raise BadAttributeFormat()\n 764 | \n 765 |         # Extracts the raw name and type\n 766 |         name, type_ = m.groups()\n 767 | \n 768 |         # Extracts the final name\n 769 |         name = unicode(name.strip('\"\\''))\n 770 | \n 771 |         # Extracts the final type\n 772 |         if _RE_TYPE_NOMINAL.match(type_):\n 773 |             try:\n 774 |                 type_ = _parse_values(type_.strip('{} '))\n 775 |             except Exception:\n 776 |                 raise BadAttributeType()\n 777 |             if isinstance(type_, dict):\n 778 |                 raise BadAttributeType()\n 779 | \n 780 |         else:\n 781 |             # If not nominal, verify the type name\n 782 |             type_ = unicode(type_).upper()\n 783 |             if type_ not in ['NUMERIC', 'REAL', 'INTEGER', 'STRING']:\n 784 |                 raise BadAttributeType()\n 785 | \n 786 |         return (name, type_)\n 787 | \n 788 |     def _decode(self, s, encode_nominal=False, matrix_type=DENSE):\n 789 |         '''Do the job the ``encode``.'''\n 790 | \n 791 |         # Make sure this method is idempotent\n 792 |         self._current_line = 0\n 793 | \n 794 |         # If string, convert to a list of lines\n 795 |         if isinstance(s, basestring):\n 796 |             s = s.strip('\\r\\n ').replace('\\r\\n', '\\n').split('\\n')\n 797 | \n 798 |         # Create the return object\n 799 |         obj: ArffContainerType = {\n 800 |             u'description': u'',\n 801 |             u'relation': u'',\n 802 |             u'attributes': [],\n 803 |             u'data': []\n 804 |         }\n 805 |         attribute_names = {}\n 806 | \n 807 |         # Create the data helper object\n 808 |         data = _get_data_object_for_decoding(matrix_type)\n 809 | \n 810 |         # Read all lines\n 811 |         STATE = _TK_DESCRIPTION\n 812 |         s = iter(s)\n 813 |         for row in s:\n 814 |             self._current_line += 1\n 815 |             # Ignore empty lines\n 816 |             row = row.strip(' \\r\\n')\n 817 |             if not row: continue\n 818 | \n 819 |             u_row = row.upper()\n 820 | \n 821 |             # DESCRIPTION -----------------------------------------------------\n 822 |             if u_row.startswith(_TK_DESCRIPTION) and STATE == _TK_DESCRIPTION:\n 823 |                 obj['description'] += self._decode_comment(row) + '\\n'\n 824 |             # -----------------------------------------------------------------\n 825 | \n 826 |             # RELATION --------------------------------------------------------\n 827 |             elif u_row.startswith(_TK_RELATION):\n 828 |                 if STATE != _TK_DESCRIPTION:\n 829 |                     raise BadLayout()\n 830 | \n 831 |                 STATE = _TK_RELATION\n 832 |                 obj['relation'] = self._decode_relation(row)\n 833 |             # -----------------------------------------------------------------\n 834 | \n 835 |             # ATTRIBUTE -------------------------------------------------------\n 836 |             elif u_row.startswith(_TK_ATTRIBUTE):\n 837 |                 if STATE != _TK_RELATION and STATE != _TK_ATTRIBUTE:\n 838 |                     raise BadLayout()\n 839 | \n 840 |                 STATE = _TK_ATTRIBUTE\n 841 | \n 842 |                 attr = self._decode_attribute(row)\n 843 |                 if attr[0] in attribute_names:\n 844 |                     raise BadAttributeName(attr[0], attribute_names[attr[0]])\n 845 |                 else:\n 846 |                     attribute_names[attr[0]] = self._current_line\n 847 |                 obj['attributes'].append(attr)\n 848 | \n 849 |                 if isinstance(attr[1], (list, tuple)):\n 850 |                     if encode_nominal:\n 851 |                         conversor = EncodedNominalConversor(attr[1])\n 852 |                     else:\n 853 |                         conversor = NominalConversor(attr[1])\n 854 |                 else:\n 855 |                     CONVERSOR_MAP = {'STRING': unicode,\n 856 |                                      'INTEGER': lambda x: int(float(x)),\n 857 |                                      'NUMERIC': float,\n 858 |                                      'REAL': float}\n 859 |                     conversor = CONVERSOR_MAP[attr[1]]\n 860 | \n 861 |                 self._conversors.append(conversor)\n 862 |             # -----------------------------------------------------------------\n 863 | \n 864 |             # DATA ------------------------------------------------------------\n 865 |             elif u_row.startswith(_TK_DATA):\n 866 |                 if STATE != _TK_ATTRIBUTE:\n 867 |                     raise BadLayout()\n 868 | \n 869 |                 break\n 870 |             # -----------------------------------------------------------------\n 871 | \n 872 |             # COMMENT ---------------------------------------------------------\n 873 |             elif u_row.startswith(_TK_COMMENT):\n 874 |                 pass\n 875 |             # -----------------------------------------------------------------\n 876 |         else:\n 877 |             # Never found @DATA\n 878 |             raise BadLayout()\n 879 | \n 880 |         def stream():\n 881 |             for row in s:\n 882 |                 self._current_line += 1\n 883 |                 row = row.strip()\n 884 |                 # Ignore empty lines and comment lines.\n 885 |                 if row and not row.startswith(_TK_COMMENT):\n 886 |                     yield row\n 887 | \n 888 |         # Alter the data object\n 889 |         obj['data'] = data.decode_rows(stream(), self._conversors)\n 890 |         if obj['description'].endswith('\\n'):\n 891 |             obj['description'] = obj['description'][:-1]\n 892 | \n 893 |         return obj\n 894 | \n 895 |     def decode(self, s, encode_nominal=False, return_type=DENSE):\n 896 |         '''Returns the Python representation of a given ARFF file.\n 897 | \n 898 |         When a file object is passed as an argument, this method reads lines\n 899 |         iteratively, avoiding to load unnecessary information to the memory.\n 900 | \n 901 |         :param s: a string or file object with the ARFF file.\n 902 |         :param encode_nominal: boolean, if True perform a label encoding\n 903 |             while reading the .arff file.\n 904 |         :param return_type: determines the data structure used to store the\n 905 |             dataset. Can be one of `arff.DENSE`, `arff.COO`, `arff.LOD`,\n 906 |             `arff.DENSE_GEN` or `arff.LOD_GEN`.\n 907 |             Consult the sections on `working with sparse data`_ and `loading\n 908 |             progressively`_.\n 909 |         '''\n 910 |         try:\n 911 |             return self._decode(s, encode_nominal=encode_nominal,\n 912 |                                 matrix_type=return_type)\n 913 |         except ArffException as e:\n 914 |             e.line = self._current_line\n 915 |             raise e\n 916 | \n 917 | \n 918 | class ArffEncoder(object):\n 919 |     '''An ARFF encoder.'''\n 920 | \n 921 |     def _encode_comment(self, s=''):\n 922 |         '''(INTERNAL) Encodes a comment line.\n 923 | \n 924 |         Comments are single line strings starting, obligatorily, with the ``%``\n 925 |         character, and can have any symbol, including whitespaces or special\n 926 |         characters.\n 927 | \n 928 |         If ``s`` is None, this method will simply return an empty comment.\n 929 | \n 930 |         :param s: (OPTIONAL) string.\n 931 |         :return: a string with the encoded comment line.\n 932 |         '''\n 933 |         if s:\n 934 |             return u'%s %s'%(_TK_COMMENT, s)\n 935 |         else:\n 936 |             return u'%s' % _TK_COMMENT\n 937 | \n 938 |     def _encode_relation(self, name):\n 939 |         '''(INTERNAL) Decodes a relation line.\n 940 | \n 941 |         The relation declaration is a line with the format ``@RELATION\n 942 |         <relation-name>``, where ``relation-name`` is a string.\n 943 | \n 944 |         :param name: a string.\n 945 |         :return: a string with the encoded relation declaration.\n 946 |         '''\n 947 |         for char in ' %{},':\n 948 |             if char in name:\n 949 |                 name = '\"%s\"'%name\n 950 |                 break\n 951 | \n 952 |         return u'%s %s'%(_TK_RELATION, name)\n 953 | \n 954 |     def _encode_attribute(self, name, type_):\n 955 |         '''(INTERNAL) Encodes an attribute line.\n 956 | \n 957 |         The attribute follow the template::\n 958 | \n 959 |              @attribute <attribute-name> <datatype>\n 960 | \n 961 |         where ``attribute-name`` is a string, and ``datatype`` can be:\n 962 | \n 963 |         - Numerical attributes as ``NUMERIC``, ``INTEGER`` or ``REAL``.\n 964 |         - Strings as ``STRING``.\n 965 |         - Dates (NOT IMPLEMENTED).\n 966 |         - Nominal attributes with format:\n 967 | \n 968 |             {<nominal-name1>, <nominal-name2>, <nominal-name3>, ...}\n 969 | \n 970 |         This method must receive a the name of the attribute and its type, if\n 971 |         the attribute type is nominal, ``type`` must be a list of values.\n 972 | \n 973 |         :param name: a string.\n 974 |         :param type_: a string or a list of string.\n 975 |         :return: a string with the encoded attribute declaration.\n 976 |         '''\n 977 |         for char in ' %{},':\n 978 |             if char in name:\n 979 |                 name = '\"%s\"'%name\n 980 |                 break\n 981 | \n 982 |         if isinstance(type_, (tuple, list)):\n 983 |             type_tmp = [u'%s' % encode_string(type_k) for type_k in type_]\n 984 |             type_ = u'{%s}'%(u', '.join(type_tmp))\n 985 | \n 986 |         return u'%s %s %s'%(_TK_ATTRIBUTE, name, type_)\n 987 | \n 988 |     def encode(self, obj):\n 989 |         '''Encodes a given object to an ARFF file.\n 990 | \n 991 |         :param obj: the object containing the ARFF information.\n 992 |         :return: the ARFF file as an unicode string.\n 993 |         '''\n 994 |         data = [row for row in self.iter_encode(obj)]\n 995 | \n 996 |         return u'\\n'.join(data)\n 997 | \n 998 |     def iter_encode(self, obj):\n 999 |         '''The iterative version of `arff.ArffEncoder.encode`.\n1000 | \n1001 |         This encodes iteratively a given object and return, one-by-one, the\n1002 |         lines of the ARFF file.\n1003 | \n1004 |         :param obj: the object containing the ARFF information.\n1005 |         :return: (yields) the ARFF file as unicode strings.\n1006 |         '''\n1007 |         # DESCRIPTION\n1008 |         if obj.get('description', None):\n1009 |             for row in obj['description'].split('\\n'):\n1010 |                 yield self._encode_comment(row)\n1011 | \n1012 |         # RELATION\n1013 |         if not obj.get('relation'):\n1014 |             raise BadObject('Relation name not found or with invalid value.')\n1015 | \n1016 |         yield self._encode_relation(obj['relation'])\n1017 |         yield u''\n1018 | \n1019 |         # ATTRIBUTES\n1020 |         if not obj.get('attributes'):\n1021 |             raise BadObject('Attributes not found.')\n1022 | \n1023 |         attribute_names = set()\n1024 |         for attr in obj['attributes']:\n1025 |             # Verify for bad object format\n1026 |             if not isinstance(attr, (tuple, list)) or \\\n1027 |                len(attr) != 2 or \\\n1028 |                not isinstance(attr[0], basestring):\n1029 |                 raise BadObject('Invalid attribute declaration \"%s\"'%str(attr))\n1030 | \n1031 |             if isinstance(attr[1], basestring):\n1032 |                 # Verify for invalid types\n1033 |                 if attr[1] not in _SIMPLE_TYPES:\n1034 |                     raise BadObject('Invalid attribute type \"%s\"'%str(attr))\n1035 | \n1036 |             # Verify for bad object format\n1037 |             elif not isinstance(attr[1], (tuple, list)):\n1038 |                 raise BadObject('Invalid attribute type \"%s\"'%str(attr))\n1039 | \n1040 |             # Verify attribute name is not used twice\n1041 |             if attr[0] in attribute_names:\n1042 |                 raise BadObject('Trying to use attribute name \"%s\" for the '\n1043 |                                 'second time.' % str(attr[0]))\n1044 |             else:\n1045 |                 attribute_names.add(attr[0])\n1046 | \n1047 |             yield self._encode_attribute(attr[0], attr[1])\n1048 |         yield u''\n1049 |         attributes = obj['attributes']\n1050 | \n1051 |         # DATA\n1052 |         yield _TK_DATA\n1053 |         if 'data' in obj:\n1054 |             data = _get_data_object_for_encoding(obj.get('data'))\n1055 |             for line in data.encode_data(obj.get('data'), attributes):\n1056 |                 yield line\n1057 | \n1058 |         yield u''\n1059 | \n1060 | # =============================================================================\n1061 | \n1062 | # BASIC INTERFACE =============================================================\n1063 | def load(fp, encode_nominal=False, return_type=DENSE):\n1064 |     '''Load a file-like object containing the ARFF document and convert it into\n1065 |     a Python object.\n1066 | \n1067 |     :param fp: a file-like object.\n1068 |     :param encode_nominal: boolean, if True perform a label encoding\n1069 |         while reading the .arff file.\n1070 |     :param return_type: determines the data structure used to store the\n1071 |         dataset. Can be one of `arff.DENSE`, `arff.COO`, `arff.LOD`,\n1072 |         `arff.DENSE_GEN` or `arff.LOD_GEN`.\n1073 |         Consult the sections on `working with sparse data`_ and `loading\n1074 |         progressively`_.\n1075 |     :return: a dictionary.\n1076 |      '''\n1077 |     decoder = ArffDecoder()\n1078 |     return decoder.decode(fp, encode_nominal=encode_nominal,\n1079 |                           return_type=return_type)\n1080 | \n1081 | def loads(s, encode_nominal=False, return_type=DENSE):\n1082 |     '''Convert a string instance containing the ARFF document into a Python\n1083 |     object.\n1084 | \n1085 |     :param s: a string object.\n1086 |     :param encode_nominal: boolean, if True perform a label encoding\n1087 |         while reading the .arff file.\n1088 |     :param return_type: determines the data structure used to store the\n1089 |         dataset. Can be one of `arff.DENSE`, `arff.COO`, `arff.LOD`,\n1090 |         `arff.DENSE_GEN` or `arff.LOD_GEN`.\n1091 |         Consult the sections on `working with sparse data`_ and `loading\n1092 |         progressively`_.\n1093 |     :return: a dictionary.\n1094 |     '''\n1095 |     decoder = ArffDecoder()\n1096 |     return decoder.decode(s, encode_nominal=encode_nominal,\n1097 |                           return_type=return_type)\n1098 | \n1099 | def dump(obj, fp):\n1100 |     '''Serialize an object representing the ARFF document to a given file-like\n1101 |     object.\n1102 | \n1103 |     :param obj: a dictionary.\n1104 |     :param fp: a file-like object.\n1105 |     '''\n1106 |     encoder = ArffEncoder()\n1107 |     generator = encoder.iter_encode(obj)\n1108 | \n1109 |     last_row = next(generator)\n1110 |     for row in generator:\n1111 |         fp.write(last_row + u'\\n')\n1112 |         last_row = row\n1113 |     fp.write(last_row)\n1114 | \n1115 |     return fp\n1116 | \n1117 | def dumps(obj):\n1118 |     '''Serialize an object representing the ARFF document, returning a string.\n1119 | \n1120 |     :param obj: a dictionary.\n1121 |     :return: a string with the ARFF document.\n1122 |     '''\n1123 |     encoder = ArffEncoder()\n1124 |     return encoder.encode(obj)\n1125 | # =============================================================================\n1126 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the analysis of the issue and the codebase exploration, I can identify the key locations that need to be modified to implement MD5 checksum validation for downloaded ARFF files. Here's the localization result:\n\n```\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 488-551\nfunction: _download_data_to_bunch\ndescription: Main function that downloads the dataset and needs checksum validation\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 508-510\nfunction: _stream_checksum_generator\ndescription: Helper function for checksum calculation\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 809-812\nfunction: fetch_openml\ndescription: Entry point that receives data_home parameter\n\nsklearn/utils/validation.py\nline: 1344-1378\nfunction: _check_fit_params\ndescription: Contains validation logic patterns\n\nsklearn/externals/_arff.py\nline: 286-293\nfunction: _parse_values\ndescription: ARFF parsing utility function\n```\n\nThe locations are ordered by importance for implementing the MD5 checksum validation feature. The core changes would need to be made in the `_download_data_to_bunch` function in `_openml.py`, which is responsible for downloading the dataset. This function would need to integrate with the `_stream_checksum_generator` function to calculate and verify the MD5 checksum.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 135094, "completion_tokens": 534}, "time": 54.963343143463135}]}}
{"instance_id": "pandas-dev__pandas-60415", "found_files": [["pandas/core/generic.py", "pandas/core/frame.py"]], "found_modules": [["pandas/core/internals/blocks.py:Block", "pandas/core/apply.py:FrameApply", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:MultipleCategories", "pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame", "pandas/conftest.py:ea_scalar_and_dtype", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:TransformNaN", "pandas/core/series.py:Series", "pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame"]], "found_entities": [["pandas/core/internals/blocks.py:Block._maybe_squeeze_arg", "pandas/core/apply.py:FrameApply.res_columns", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:MultipleCategories.time_groupby_extra_cat_sort", "pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.squeeze", "pandas/conftest.py:ea_scalar_and_dtype", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:TransformNaN.setup", "pandas/core/series.py:Series._get_rows_with_mask", "pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.axes"]], "raw_output_loc": ["After analyzing the issue and the code, I can now provide the locations that require modification to add type hints for the `squeeze` method. Here's the result in the requested format:\n\n```\npandas/core/generic.py\nline: 841\nline: 842\nline: 862\nline: 863\nfunction: NDFrame.squeeze\n\npandas/core/generic.py\nline: 944\nline: 945\nline: 946\nline: 947\nline: 948\nline: 949\nfunction: NDFrame.squeeze\n\npandas/core/generic.py\nline: 951\nline: 952\nfunction: NDFrame.squeeze\n\npandas/core/frame.py\nline: 993\nline: 1012\nclass: DataFrame\nfunction: axes\n\npandas/core/generic.py\nline: 861\nline: 862\nline: 863\nline: 864\nfunction: NDFrame.squeeze\n```\n\nThe issue requires adding type hints for the `squeeze` method in `pandas/core/generic.py`. The main changes needed are:\n\n1. Add type annotations for the method signature (lines 841-864)\n2. Update the docstring to include return type annotations\n3. Modify the implementation details to handle type annotations consistently with the rest of the codebase\n\nThe core `squeeze` method in `generic.py` is the primary location needing attention. The method is used extensively throughout the codebase, particularly in:\n- DataFrame operations\n- Plotting utilities\n- Grouping and aggregation operations\n- Various core pandas components\n\nThe type hint addition should follow the existing patterns in the codebase, particularly in similar methods and across the dependencies we've analyzed."], "meta_data": {"repo": "pandas-dev/pandas", "base_commit": "98f7e4deeff26a5ef993ee27104387a1a6e0d3d3", "problem_statement": "DOC: Missing type hint for squeeze method\n### Pandas version checks\n\n- [X] I have checked that the issue still exists on the latest versions of the docs on `main` [here](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/)\n\n\n### Location of the documentation\n\nhttps://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/blob/main/pandas/core/generic.py\n\n### Documentation problem\n\nThe squeeze method is missing a type hint. \n\n### Suggested fix for documentation\n\nAdding a type hint to the squeeze method to be consistent with the rest of the code. \n", "patch": "diff --git a/pandas/core/generic.py b/pandas/core/generic.py\nindex 039bdf9c36ee7..a6be17a654aa7 100644\n--- a/pandas/core/generic.py\n+++ b/pandas/core/generic.py\n@@ -838,7 +838,7 @@ def pop(self, item: Hashable) -> Series | Any:\n         return result\n \n     @final\n-    def squeeze(self, axis: Axis | None = None):\n+    def squeeze(self, axis: Axis | None = None) -> Scalar | Series | DataFrame:\n         \"\"\"\n         Squeeze 1 dimensional axis objects into scalars.\n \n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 71273, "completion_tokens": 573}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to